var e=JSON.parse('[[2201,"`useState` is used to hold data whose change ought to cause the component to re-render. `useRef` holds a mutable value whose change does not cause a re-render. `useRef` is often used to access DOM elements or to hold technical values (timers, previous values). The choice depends on whether a change of the value affects the display of the UI.","Although `useRef` and `useState` can both hold data between renders, their purposes are different.\\n\\nA definition\\n\\n- `useState` — a hook for holding state that affects the UI.\\n- `useRef` — a hook for holding a mutable reference that does not affect the render.\\n\\nuseState\\n\\nThe characteristics\\n\\n- A change of state causes a repeated render.\\n- It is used for data displayed in the UI.\\n- The updates can be batched.\\n\\nuseRef\\n\\nThe characteristics\\n\\n- The value is held in `ref.current`.\\n- Changing `current` does not cause a render.\\n- The value is preserved between renders.\\n\\nThe typical scenarios for useRef\\n\\n- Access to DOM elements.\\n- Holding the id of a `setTimeout` / `setInterval`.\\n- Holding the previous value of state.\\n- Flags and counters that do not affect the UI.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useState` is for interface data; `useRef` is for technical and auxiliary values that should not cause a re-render.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nsetCount(count + 1); // it causes a re-render\\n\\nconst renderCount = useRef(0);\\n\\nrenderCount.current += 1; // no re-render is caused"],[2202,"`useEffect` is for performing side effects in functional components. It is used for working with external systems: APIs, timers, subscriptions, the DOM. `useEffect` runs after the component has rendered. By means of the array of dependencies you can control when the effect is to run.","Functional components ought in themselves to be pure, and all of the \\"side\\" logic is moved into `useEffect`.\\n\\nA definition\\nA side effect is any operation that interacts with the outside world or changes state outside the current render.\\n\\nWhat is normally done in useEffect\\n\\n- Loading data.\\n- Subscribing to events.\\n- Timers and intervals.\\n- Working with the DOM.\\n- Logging.\\n\\nAn example of loading data\\n\\nThe array of dependencies\\n\\n- Without the array — the effect is called after every render.\\n- An empty array `[]` — the effect is called once.\\n- With dependencies — when the values given change.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useEffect` is the main tool for working with side effects and for managing the lifecycle in functional components.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData();\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(count);\\n}, [count]);"],[2203,"`useEffect` is called once if it is given an empty array of dependencies `[]`. In that case the effect runs after the component\'s first render. This behaviour is equivalent to `componentDidMount` in class components. In `StrictMode` in development mode, however, the effect may be called twice.","An empty array of dependencies tells React that the effect depends on no values at all.\\n\\nA definition\\nAn empty array of dependencies (`[]`) means the effect should not be recomputed when the state or the props update.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nAn important subtlety: StrictMode\\nIn React StrictMode (in dev mode only):\\n- The component mounts twice.\\n- The effect is called twice.\\n- That is done in order to find side effects.\\n\\nHow to write effects safely\\n\\n- Make your effects idempotent.\\n- Use a cleanup function.\\n- Do not rely on a single call in dev mode.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useEffect` with an empty array is called once, when the component mounts, but in development mode with `StrictMode` that behaviour can differ.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"mounted\\");\\n}, []);"],[2204,"First React performs the `render`, then at the commit stage `useLayoutEffect` is called, and only after the browser has painted is `useEffect` called. `useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously and blocks the paint, whereas `useEffect` runs asynchronously, after the paint. `useLayoutEffect` is therefore used for working with layout, and `useEffect` for side effects that do not affect the markup.","React divides the updating of the UI into several phases, and the hooks are tied to those phases.\\n\\nA definition\\n\\n- Render — computing the JSX and the virtual tree.\\n- The commit phase — applying the changes to the DOM.\\n- Paint — the browser painting.\\n\\nThe order of execution\\n\\n- `render`\\n- The component\'s function is called.\\n- The JSX is computed.\\n- The DOM has not been updated yet.\\n\\n- The commit phase\\n- The real DOM is updated.\\n- `useLayoutEffect` is called.\\n\\n- Paint\\n- The browser paints the updated UI.\\n\\n- `useEffect`\\n- It runs after the paint.\\n\\nVisually\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect\\n\\nThe characteristics\\n\\n- It runs synchronously.\\n- It blocks the paint.\\n- It is suited to measurements and to synchronous changes to the DOM.\\n\\nuseEffect\\n\\nThe characteristics\\n\\n- It does not block the paint.\\n- It runs after the paint.\\n- It is preferable in most cases.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useLayoutEffect` is needed when it matters to intervene before the paint, and `useEffect` when the effect does not affect the layout.","render → DOM update → useLayoutEffect → paint → useEffect\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  // measuring the DOM\'s dimensions\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  // requests, subscriptions, logic\\n}, []);"],[2205,"A cleanup function is implemented by returning a function from `useEffect`. That function is called before the effect runs again and when the component unmounts. Cleanup is used to clear timers, subscriptions and event listeners. It prevents memory leaks and incorrect behaviour.","Any effect that \\"subscribes\\" to something or creates a resource has to be able to clean it up.\\n\\nA definition\\nA cleanup function is a function returned from `useEffect`, intended for cleaning up a side effect.\\n\\nAn example with a subscription\\n\\nAn example with a timer\\n\\nWhen cleanup is mandatory\\n\\n- Events (`addEventListener`).\\n- Timers (`setTimeout`, `setInterval`).\\n- Subscriptions (WebSocket, RxJS).\\n- Third-party libraries.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA cleanup function is an essential part of a correct `useEffect`, if the effect creates long-lived resources.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const handler = () => console.log(\\"resize\\");\\n  window.addEventListener(\\"resize\\", handler);\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    window.removeEventListener(\\"resize\\", handler);\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    console.log(\\"tick\\");\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);"],[2206,"The cleanup function is called before the effect runs again and when the component unmounts. If the effect has an empty array of dependencies, the cleanup will be called only on unmount. In `StrictMode` in dev mode the cleanup may be called more often. That is normal behaviour.","Cleanup is part of an effect\'s lifecycle, and it is tied strictly to the dependencies.\\n\\nA definition\\nCleanup is the logic for cleaning an effect up before it runs again or before the component is removed.\\n\\nThe scenarios in which cleanup is called\\n\\n- The component unmounts\\n- The component is removed from the DOM.\\n\\n- The dependencies change\\n- The old effect is cleaned up.\\n- Then the new effect runs.\\n\\n- StrictMode (dev)\\n- The effect and the cleanup are called additionally, as a check.\\n\\nAn example with a dependency\\n\\nThe behaviour\\n\\n- `id` changes.\\n- The cleanup for the old `id` is performed.\\n- The effect for the new `id` runs.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe cleanup function is always called before an effect is destroyed — either because the dependencies changed, or when the component unmounts.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"effect for\\", id);\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    console.log(\\"cleanup for\\", id);\\n  };\\n}, [id]);"],[2207,"A component\'s mount and unmount are tracked by means of `useEffect`. An effect with an empty array of dependencies `[]` runs on mounting. That effect\'s cleanup function is called on unmounting. This is the functional equivalent of `componentDidMount` and `componentWillUnmount`.","In functional components React\'s lifecycle is controlled through `useEffect`.\\n\\nA definition\\n\\n- Mount — the moment when the component is first added to the DOM.\\n- Unmount — the moment when the component is removed from the DOM.\\n\\nTracking the mount\\n\\nWhy this works\\n\\n- An empty array of dependencies means the effect runs only after the first render.\\n- The effect runs once (except in StrictMode in dev).\\n\\nTracking the unmount\\n\\nWhat happens\\n\\n- React calls the cleanup function.\\n- The component is removed from the DOM.\\n- Every resource associated with it has to be cleaned up.\\n\\nThe frequent use cases\\n\\n- Logging the lifecycle.\\n- Subscribing / unsubscribing.\\n- Initializing and cleaning up third-party libraries.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useEffect` with an empty array of dependencies is the standard, reliable way of tracking a component\'s mount and unmount.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"component mounted\\");\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  return () => {\\n    console.log(\\"component unmounted\\");\\n  };\\n}, []);"],[2208,"State in React can be held locally in a component, lifted up the component tree, held in a context, or held in external store managers. The choice depends on the state\'s scope and on the number of components that need it. The wider the area in which the state is used, the higher the level at which it should be held. There is no universal solution — what matters is the balance between simplicity and scalability.","React does not impose a single way of holding state but provides several levels.\\n\\nA definition\\nState is data that affects the display of the UI and can change over time.\\n\\nThe main ways of holding state\\n\\n1. A component\'s local state\\n\\nIt is used when:\\n- The state is needed by only one component.\\n- It is directly related to that component\'s UI.\\n\\n2. Lifted state (lifting state up)\\nThe state is held in the nearest common parent and passed down through props.\\n\\nIt is used when:\\n- Several components have to stay in sync.\\n- Shared management of the data is needed.\\n\\n3. The Context API\\n\\nIt is used when:\\n- The data is needed by many components.\\n- You do not want to drill props through several levels.\\n\\n4. Global state (stores)\\nExamples: Redux, Zustand, MobX (conceptually).\\n\\nIt is used when:\\n- The application\'s state is complex.\\n- There is a lot of logic and business rules.\\n- Centralized management is needed.\\n\\n5. The URL as state\\n\\n- Query parameters.\\n- Route parameters.\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n- Navigation.\\n- Sharing state through a link.\\n\\nConclusion\\nState should be held as close as possible to where it is used, and lifted higher only when necessary.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext();"],[2209,"By \\"the React+TS stack\\" people normally mean not only React and TypeScript but the build, the routing, the state management, the handling of requests, the styles, the testing and the code quality too. I would list what I have really worked with: Vite/Webpack, React Router, Redux Toolkit or React Query, ESLint/Prettier, Jest/Vitest/RTL, CSS Modules or styled-components, for example. It is important to say which versions and which approaches you applied (hooks, strict TS, feature-sliced or another architectural style, for example). And be sure to add what you did with your own hands: configuring the configs, migrating, optimizing, maintaining.","By \\"the stack\\" people most often mean the full set of solutions around a React application. It is convenient to answer in \\"layers\\", in order to show that you think systematically.\\n\\nWhat the stack normally comprises\\n\\n- The application\'s base\\n- `React` (hooks, functional components)\\n- `TypeScript` (strict mode, typing the props/hooks/API)\\n\\n- The build and the dev environment\\n- `Vite` or `Webpack`\\n- working with `env` variables, aliases, code splitting\\n\\n- Routing\\n- `React Router` (nested routes, guards, lazy pages)\\n\\n- Data and state\\n- server state: `React Query` / `SWR`\\n- global state: `Redux Toolkit`, sometimes `Zustand` or `MobX`\\n- local state: `useState`, `useReducer`, `useContext`\\n\\n- Styles\\n- `CSS Modules`, `SCSS`, `styled-components`, `Emotion`, `Vanilla Extract`\\n- design systems: `MUI`, `Ant Design` (if you have used them)\\n\\n- Code quality and tests\\n- `ESLint`, `Prettier`, `Husky`/pre-commit\\n- `Vitest/Jest`, `React Testing Library`, sometimes `Cypress/Playwright`\\n\\nHow to answer this \\"like a grown-up\\" in an interview\\nBefore the list, add a sentence or two about the context, so that it sounds like experience rather than \\"a list of libraries\\":\\n- The project context\\n- \\"I built an SPA/a customer account area/an admin panel/a storefront, where forms, tables and query caching mattered…\\"\\n\\n- What you did yourself\\n- \\"I configured the lint/the build, migrated to TS strict, optimized the bundle, introduced RTK Query/React Query…\\"\\n\\n- Why you chose the approach\\n- \\"For server data I used React Query because of the cache and the refetching, and kept Redux for the UI/global flags…\\"\\n\\nConclusion\\nIn answering this question it is better to show not breadth — \\"I know a hundred libraries\\" — but the links: what stack the project had + what you did in it + why it was chosen that way.",null],[2210,"The simplest way is to hold the \\"elapsed seconds\\" in `useState` and to start the updates in `useEffect` by means of `setInterval`. Inside the interval, increment the value every second through the functional update `setState(prev => prev + 1)`. You must return a clean-up function in order to stop the interval on unmounting. For a more accurate stopwatch it is better to hold the `startTime` in a `useRef` and to recompute the elapsed time from the real clock, so as to reduce the \\"drift\\".","A stopwatch can be made \\"simple\\" (approximate) or \\"more accurate\\" (based on the real clock).\\n\\nOption 1: a simple stopwatch (incrementing once a second)\\nThe idea is simple: once every 1000 ms we increment a counter.\\n\\n- Why `setSeconds((s) => s + 1)` is used\\n- it protects you from a \\"stale value\\" in the closure and works reliably.\\n\\nOption 2: a more accurate stopwatch (less drift)\\nIntervals in the browser can \\"drift\\" (the tab is inactive, the system is loaded, the timers are throttled). You can therefore hold the start time and compute the elapsed time from `Date.now()`.\\n\\n- What `useRef` gives you\\n- the value of `startRef.current` is preserved between renders and does not itself cause a re-render.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor simple UI tasks incrementing once a second is enough, but for a \\"real\\" stopwatch it is better to count the seconds from the real clock, so that the timer drifts less.","import { useEffect, useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nexport function Stopwatch() {\\n  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = window.setInterval(() => {\\n      setSeconds((s) => s + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => window.clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>{seconds}s</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useEffect, useRef, useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nexport function StopwatchAccurate() {\\n  const startRef = useRef<number>(Date.now());\\n  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = window.setInterval(() => {\\n      const diffMs = Date.now() - startRef.current;\\n      setSeconds(Math.floor(diffMs / 1000));\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    return () => window.clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>{seconds}s</div>;\\n}"],[2211,"`setInterval` goes on running even after the component has disappeared from the page, because it lives independently of React. If the interval is not cleared it will go on running its code, consuming resources, and it may try to update the `state` of a component that has already unmounted. That leads to unnecessary computation, strange bugs and warnings in the console. Timers therefore have to be stopped in the `useEffect` clean-up function.","In React any \\"external\\" actions (timers, subscriptions, WebSockets) have to be ended properly when the component is no longer needed.\\n\\nA definition\\nThe clean-up of an effect is the function React calls when the component unmounts (and before the effect runs again), in order to stop the side processes: timers, subscriptions, event listeners.\\n\\nWhat will happen if you do not clear a `setInterval`\\n\\n- An unnecessary load\\n- the interval will go on firing indefinitely, even if the UI is no longer displayed.\\n\\n- A leak of resources\\n- in time a great many \\"hanging\\" intervals can accumulate on the page.\\n\\n- Attempts to update state after the unmount\\n- if there is a `setState` inside the interval, it will be called \\"into the void\\", which often leads to warnings/unexpected behaviour.\\n\\n- Hidden bugs\\n- the interval may update a shared object/store, for example, and break the logic of other parts of the application.\\n\\nHow this is done properly in React\\nYou have to return a clean-up function from `useEffect`:\\n\\nConclusion\\n`setInterval` is a side effect that React does not manage automatically, so it has to be stopped on unmount, in order to avoid leaks and state updates \\"after the component\'s death\\".","useEffect(() => {\\n  const id = window.setInterval(() => {\\n    // ...do the work\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => window.clearInterval(id);\\n}, []);"],[2212,"A `setInterval` is cleared through `clearInterval`, called in the function that `useEffect` returns. That function runs when the component unmounts and before the effect runs again. It is important to keep the interval\'s identifier in a variable inside the effect. This approach guarantees that the timer will not go on running after the component has left the page.","React expects every effect to manage its own lifecycle, including ending properly.\\n\\nA definition\\nA cleanup function in useEffect is a function returned from `useEffect`, which React calls when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again.\\n\\nThe basic pattern for clearing an interval\\nBefore using an interval, the following pattern is normally enough:\\n\\nWhy exactly like this\\n\\n- The interval is created inside the effect\\n- React understands clearly which effect this side effect belongs to.\\n\\n- The clean-up is returned from the same effect\\n- that guarantees symmetry: what you created is what you close.\\n\\n- The effect\'s dependencies control its recreation\\n- when the dependencies change, the old interval will be cleared before the new one is created.\\n\\nThe typical mistake\\n\\n- Clearing the interval outside `useEffect`\\n- React does not know when to do it.\\n\\n- Forgetting the clean-up when there are dependencies\\n- the result is that several intervals are created at once.\\n\\nConclusion\\nClearing a `setInterval` properly in `useEffect` is an essential practice when working with timers and other side effects; otherwise an application quickly starts behaving unstably.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = window.setInterval(() => {\\n    // the interval\'s logic\\n  }, 1000);\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    window.clearInterval(intervalId);\\n  };\\n}, []);"],[2213,"If the interval is not cleared, it will go on running even after the component has unmounted. That will lead to an unnecessary load on the browser and to possible attempts to update the state of a component that no longer exists. In time such intervals accumulate and cause resource leaks. In complex applications that can lead to bugs that are hard to catch.","Although the component disappears visually, the JavaScript code associated with the interval goes on living.\\n\\nThe main consequences\\n\\n- A leak of resources\\n- the interval goes on running in the background to no purpose whatever.\\n\\n- Unnecessary computation\\n- the code inside the `setInterval` runs indefinitely.\\n\\n- Attempts to update state\\n- if there is a `setState` inside it, it will be called after the `unmount`.\\n\\n- Unstable behaviour of the application\\n- shared objects, stores or caches may be updated.\\n\\nWhy React does not stop the interval itself\\n\\n- `setInterval` is a browser API, not React.\\n- React manages the renders but does not know which timers you have started.\\n- The responsibility for ending the effect lies with the developer.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn uncleaned interval is the classic example of a resource leak and one of the most frequent mistakes when working with `useEffect`.",null],[2214,"React does not track changes to objects outside its `state` and `props`, so it does not respond to them directly. For the component to update, React has to be notified of the changes explicitly: through a subscription, through an update to local `state`, or by using special hooks. In practice `useSyncExternalStore`, a subscription system of your own, or state managers are used for this. The main thing is that a change to the external object must trigger a React re-render.","React responds only to changes that go through its update mechanism.\\n\\nWhy React does not see the external object\\nBefore talking about the solution, it is important to understand the cause:\\n- React tracks only `state` and `props`.\\n- Mutations of ordinary JS objects do not cause a re-render.\\n- An external object can change without React\'s knowledge.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\nBefore using an approach you have to choose a synchronization strategy.\\n\\n1. A subscription + local state\\n\\n- The external object reports its own changes.\\n- `setState` is called in the component in order to trigger a re-render.\\n\\n2. useSyncExternalStore\\n\\n- React provides an official API for external stores.\\n- It works correctly with concurrent rendering and strict mode.\\n- The component updates automatically when the store changes.\\n\\n3. State managers\\n\\n- Redux, MobX and Zustand encapsulate the subscriptions inside themselves.\\n- The component does not have to manage the updates by hand.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor a React component to respond to changes in an external object, those changes have to be tied explicitly to React\'s render mechanism, most often through subscriptions or `useSyncExternalStore`.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const unsubscribe = externalStore.subscribe(() => {\\n    setTick((t) => t + 1); // we force a re-render\\n  });\\n\\n  return unsubscribe;\\n}, []);"],[2215,"React re-renders only when a component\'s `state` or `props` change. Changing an ordinary JavaScript object outside React happens \\"past\\" its update system. React does not track mutations and does not subscribe to changes in external objects. Such changes therefore remain invisible to it and do not lead to a repaint.","React deliberately restricts the sources of a render, so that an application\'s behaviour is predictable.\\n\\nHow React decides when to re-render\\nBefore the list it is important to fix the basic idea: React is a reaction to changes in its input data.\\n- A re-render happens when `state` changes.\\n- A re-render happens when `props` change.\\n- Everything else React ignores.\\n\\nWhy a mutation of an external object is not taken into account\\n\\n- There is no subscription\\n- React does not know that the object has changed.\\n\\n- There is no change of reference\\n- even if the object is used in the component, its reference stays the same.\\n\\n- There is no setState call\\n- React has received no signal that an update is needed.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nConclusion\\nChanging an object outside React does not cause a re-render, because React responds not to mutations but to managed changes through state and props.","const externalConfig = { theme: \\"light\\" };\\n\\n// somewhere in the code\\nexternalConfig.theme = \\"dark\\";\\n// React will not learn about it"],[2216,"Immutability lets React understand easily and quickly that the data has changed. When a new object is created the reference changes, and React can detect that by comparing references. That makes updates predictable and makes optimizations easier. With mutations there are no such guarantees, and the behaviour becomes unstable.","Immutability is not a requirement of the language but a deliberate architectural choice by React.\\n\\nA definition\\nThe immutability of state is the approach in which the state is not changed directly but replaced by a new object with the updated data.\\n\\nWhy React needs it\\nBefore the list it is important to understand the aim: determining changes simply and quickly.\\n- Comparison by reference\\n- `prevState !== nextState` — a fast, cheap way of telling that there are changes.\\n\\n- Predictability\\n- the state cannot \\"change by itself\\" somewhere deep in the application.\\n\\n- Optimizations\\n- `memo`, `PureComponent` and `useCallback` work precisely on the change of references.\\n\\n- Safety\\n- the old renders are not broken by sudden mutations.\\n\\nWhat it would be like without immutability\\n\\n- Deep comparisons would have to be made.\\n- Performance would drop sharply.\\n- The UI\'s behaviour would become hard to explain.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact relies on immutability because the change of references makes detecting changes fast, predictable and scalable.",null],[2217,"`useState` compares the old and the new value by reference. If the reference has not changed, React considers that the state has stayed the same. When an object is mutated the reference stays the same, so no re-render is started. For React to update, a new object has to be created.","The mechanism of `useState` is directly related to the principle of immutability.\\n\\nHow React checks for a change of state\\nBefore the list it is important to fix the key idea: React does not perform a deep analysis of the data.\\n- React compares the old and the new value.\\n- The comparison is done by reference (`Object.is`).\\n- The same reference → there is no update.\\n\\nAn example of the mistake\\n\\nThe right version\\n\\n- A new object is created.\\n- The reference changes.\\n- React starts a re-render.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useState` does not respond to a mutation without a change of reference, because for React a change of state is always a replacement of the value rather than a modification of it.","const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: \\"Alex\\" });\\n\\n// bad\\nuser.name = \\"Bob\\";\\nsetUser(user); // the reference is the same → the re-render may not happen\\n\\nsetUser((prev) => ({\\n  ...prev,\\n  name: \\"Bob\\",\\n}));"],[2218,"React does not of itself know about external state, so it has to be \\"connected\\" to the render process explicitly. That is normally done by subscribing to the external store\'s changes and forcing a re-render of the component. The modern, recommended way is to use `useSyncExternalStore`. That is precisely how Redux, Zustand and the other state managers work.","The connection between external state and React\'s rendering is built around a single idea: the external store has to be able to notify React of changes.\\n\\nWhat an external store is\\nAn external store is a source of state that lives outside React (an ordinary JS object, a singleton, a class, a Redux store, a custom store).\\nThe key problem: React does not subscribe to such objects automatically.\\n\\nThe basic integration scheme\\nBefore the details it is important to fix the general algorithm:\\n- The external store holds the data.\\n- The external store is able to notify its subscribers of changes.\\n- The React component subscribes to the store.\\n- On notification React initiates a re-render.\\n\\nThe ways of connecting them\\n\\n1. A manual subscription + local state\\nThe simplest but most \\"low-level\\" approach.\\n\\n- `forceRender` is used only to start a re-render.\\n- The actual state is taken from the store.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- it is easy to make a mistake\\n- it is poorly compatible with concurrent rendering\\n\\n2. useSyncExternalStore (the recommended way)\\nReact\'s official API for external stores.\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- It works correctly in `StrictMode`.\\n- It is safe for concurrent rendering.\\n- It guarantees the consistency of the data.\\n\\n3. State managers\\nRedux, Zustand and MobX already implement this logic:\\n- The subscription is encapsulated inside the library.\\n- The components get ready-made hooks (`useSelector`, `useStore`).\\n- Unnecessary re-renders are minimized.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo connect an external store with React you should not change the data directly but build in a subscription mechanism that will initiate the re-render correctly — best of all through `useSyncExternalStore`.","function useExternalStore(store) {\\n  const [, forceRender] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    return store.subscribe(() => {\\n      forceRender((v) => v + 1);\\n    });\\n  }, [store]);\\n\\n  return store.getState();\\n}\\n\\nconst state = useSyncExternalStore(\\n  store.subscribe,\\n  store.getState\\n);"],[2219,"`useSyncExternalStore` is a React hook for connecting external state to rendering safely. It lets you subscribe to an external store and guarantees that the component always renders with the current data. This hook solves the problems that arise with manual subscriptions under concurrent rendering and Strict Mode. It is used as the basis for state managers.","`useSyncExternalStore` appeared as an answer to real architectural problems in React.\\n\\nA definition\\nuseSyncExternalStore is a hook that synchronizes an external source of data with React\'s rendering, ensuring the state is consistent in every mode React runs in.\\n\\nWhy the old approaches became a problem\\nBefore this hook was introduced, the usual approach was:\\n- A subscription in `useEffect`.\\n- Inside it — a `setState` for the re-render.\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- The data can get out of sync.\\n- It works incorrectly under concurrent rendering.\\n- Double subscriptions in `StrictMode`.\\n\\nHow useSyncExternalStore works\\nThe hook takes:\\n- A subscription function.\\n- A function for reading the current state.\\n\\nWhat happens:\\n- React reads the current state synchronously.\\n- It subscribes to changes.\\n- When something changes it schedules a re-render properly.\\n\\nWhy it is considered \\"the right way\\"\\n\\n- React itself manages the moment at which the data is read.\\n- \\"Torn\\" states between renders are ruled out.\\n- The behaviour is predictable even with interruptible renders.\\n\\nWhere it is used in practice\\n\\n- Redux (inside `useSelector`).\\n- Zustand.\\n- Any custom external store.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useSyncExternalStore` is needed in order to connect an external store with React safely and correctly, without breaking the architecture or risking bugs in the future.","const value = useSyncExternalStore(\\n  store.subscribe,\\n  store.getSnapshot\\n);"],[2220,"`useRef` lets you hold a reference to an external object between renders without starting any. Through `ref.current` you can read and change the data, but React will not repaint automatically. For the changes to be reflected in the UI, a re-render has to be initiated additionally — through `useState` or a subscription, for example. `useRef` is suited to holding references, not reactive state.","`useRef` is often used incorrectly, with people expecting the behaviour of `useState` from it.\\n\\nA definition\\nuseRef is a hook for holding a mutable value that is preserved between renders but does not affect the rendering.\\n\\nHow useRef connects an external object\\nBefore the details it is important to understand its role:\\n- `useRef` is a container for a reference.\\n- React does not watch for changes to `ref.current`.\\n\\nAn example of its use\\n\\n- The reference to the object is preserved between renders.\\n- Mutations of `objectRef.current` do not cause a re-render.\\n\\nWhen this is useful\\n\\n- Timers, WebSockets, subscriptions.\\n- A cache of values between renders.\\n- Integration with an imperative API.\\n\\nHow to reflect the changes in the UI\\nIf you need to show the changes to the user:\\n- Either tie the `useRef` to a `useState`.\\n- Or use a subscription and `useSyncExternalStore`.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useRef` is a tool for holding references and mutable objects, but not for reactive state. For UI updates it always has to be combined with React\'s render mechanisms.","function Component({ externalObject }) {\\n  const objectRef = useRef(externalObject);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    objectRef.current = externalObject;\\n  }, [externalObject]);\\n\\n  return null;\\n}\\n\\nobjectRef.current.value = 42;\\nsetTick((v) => v + 1); // we initiate a re-render"],[2221,"A subscription is implemented through a mechanism by which the external object notifies its subscribers of changes. The React component subscribes on mounting and unsubscribes on unmounting. On receiving a notification the component initiates a re-render through `setState` or another React mechanism. It is important that the subscription and the unsubscription be symmetrical.","A subscription is the basic way of \\"telling\\" React that something has changed in the outside world.\\n\\nA definition\\nA subscription is a mechanism by which an object holds a list of listeners and notifies them when its state changes.\\n\\nThe basic structure of the external object\\nThe external object has to be able to:\\n- Hold the state.\\n- Register subscribers.\\n- Notify them of changes.\\n\\nThe subscription in the React component\\n\\n- The subscription is created on mounting.\\n- The unsubscription is performed on unmounting.\\n- React receives the signal that a render is needed.\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Forgetting to unsubscribe.\\n- Creating the subscription on every render.\\n- Mutating the store without notifying the subscribers.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA subscription to an external object is an explicit contract between the external state and React, without which re-renders are impossible.","const store = {\\n  value: 0,\\n  listeners: new Set<() => void>(),\\n\\n  getState() {\\n    return this.value;\\n  },\\n\\n  setState(next: number) {\\n    this.value = next;\\n    this.listeners.forEach((l) => l());\\n  },\\n\\n  subscribe(listener: () => void) {\\n    this.listeners.add(listener);\\n    return () => this.listeners.delete(listener);\\n  },\\n};\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const unsubscribe = store.subscribe(() => {\\n    setTick((v) => v + 1); // we initiate a re-render\\n  });\\n\\n  return unsubscribe;\\n}, []);"],[2222,"A React re-render can be initiated only through React\'s own mechanisms — most often through `setState`. An external store cannot of itself repaint a component; it can only notify React. That is normally done through a subscription, inside which `setState` is called or `useSyncExternalStore` is used. There is no direct \\"force update\\" from outside.","It is important to fix the boundary straight away: external code does not control the render directly.\\n\\nThe correct ways of initiating a re-render\\n\\n1. Through local state\\nThe simplest and clearest option.\\n\\n- `forceRender` is used only as a trigger.\\n- The data is read directly from the store.\\n\\n2. Through useSyncExternalStore\\nThe recommended way.\\n\\n- React manages the subscription itself.\\n- The re-render happens automatically when something changes.\\n\\n3. Through state managers\\nRedux, MobX, Zustand:\\n- They encapsulate the subscription.\\n- They minimize unnecessary re-renders.\\n- They provide convenient hooks.\\n\\nThe incorrect approaches\\n\\n- Mutating an object and waiting for a render.\\n- Trying to trigger a re-render \\"from outside React\\".\\n- Using `useRef` as reactive state.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA React re-render can be initiated only through its state mechanisms, while an external store should merely notify of its own changes properly.","const [, forceRender] = useState(0);\\n\\nstore.subscribe(() => {\\n  forceRender((v) => v + 1);\\n});\\n\\nconst value = useSyncExternalStore(\\n  store.subscribe,\\n  store.getState\\n);"],[2223,"An embedded micro-frontend is rendered inside the existing React tree and is subject to its contexts and its lifecycle. A separate React render creates a root of its own and is entirely isolated from the parent application. The first option is easier to integrate; the second is better from the point of view of isolation and of the teams\' independence. The choice depends on the requirements for coupling and autonomy.","The difference between these approaches is in essence a difference in the level of isolation.\\n\\nAn embedded micro-frontend\\nBefore the details it is important to understand the idea: it is part of the shared React tree.\\n- It uses the shared `React root`.\\n- It inherits the `Context`, the theme, the i18n.\\n- It can receive `props` directly.\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- Simple integration.\\n- A minimum of infrastructure.\\n- A single state and design system.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- Strong coupling.\\n- Harder to update independently.\\n\\nA separate React render\\nHere each micro-frontend lives \\"on its own\\".\\n- Its own `createRoot`.\\n- Its own context and store.\\n- Communication through a public API.\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- Strict isolation.\\n- Independent releases.\\n- Less risk of breaking the host.\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- More complicated integration.\\n- Duplicated infrastructure.\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\n\\n- An embedded micro-frontend — when you need close integration and a single UX.\\n- A separate render — when the teams\' independence and deployment matter.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe difference between these approaches is in the degree of isolation and autonomy: the more independence a micro-frontend needs, the more sense a separate React render makes.",null],[2224,"Two different React trees cannot share `state` or `context` directly. To synchronize between them an external source of truth is used: an external store, a state manager or the browser\'s own mechanisms. Both trees subscribe to that source\'s changes and update when it changes. The synchronization therefore happens not through React but through a shared external layer.","It is important to start with the key limitation: every React root is entirely isolated.\\n\\nWhy it cannot be done directly\\n\\n- `Context` works only inside a single tree.\\n- `props` cannot \\"jump\\" between roots.\\n- React provides no global mechanism for synchronizing between trees.\\n\\nThe basic principle of synchronization\\nBefore the details let us fix the scheme:\\n- There is a single source of state.\\n- Both React trees read their data from it.\\n- Changes in the source notify both trees.\\n- Each tree initiates a re-render of its own.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n1. An external store\\nThe most universal and the correct option.\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- A clear architecture.\\n- Controlled updates.\\n- Scalability.\\n\\n2. State managers\\nRedux, Zustand, MobX:\\n- One store per application.\\n- Several React roots subscribe to it.\\n- The synchronization happens automatically.\\n\\n3. The browser\'s mechanisms\\nUsed more rarely, but possible:\\n- `localStorage` + the `storage` event.\\n- `BroadcastChannel`.\\n- `window.postMessage`.\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Trying to pass data directly between trees.\\n- Duplicating the state and keeping it in sync \\"by hand\\".\\n- Making cross-calls to components from different roots.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSynchronization between React trees is possible only through an external source of state, which acts as the single point of truth.","// the shared store\\nstore.setState({ theme: \\"dark\\" });\\n\\n// in each React tree\\nconst theme = useSyncExternalStore(\\n  store.subscribe,\\n  store.getState\\n);"],[2225,"In Material UI styles are most often overridden through the theme (`theme.components`), through the `sx` prop or through custom `styled` components. For global changes a theme override is used; for local ones `sx`. It is important not to break the specificity and not to override styles \\"head-on\\" through CSS. The best approach is to start with the theme and to use `sx` selectively.","Material UI offers several levels of customization — from the global to the local.\\n\\nA definition\\nA definition: overriding styles in Material UI is configuring components\' appearance through the library\'s official APIs without hacking CSS specificity.\\n\\nThe main ways of overriding\\n\\n1. Through the theme (theme overrides)\\nUsed for rules that are shared across the whole application.\\n\\n- It is suited to design systems\\n- It works reliably across updates\\n- It does not depend on the DOM structure\\n\\n2. Through the `sx` prop\\nThe best way for local edits.\\n\\n- It is applied right beside the component\\n- It uses the theme\'s tokens\\n- It requires no separate files\\n\\n3. Through `styled`\\nUsed to create reusable wrappers.\\n\\n- Good for a UI kit\\n- The styles are encapsulated\\n- It combines easily with the theme\\n\\nWhat is better avoided\\n\\n- Global CSS with `.MuiButton-root`\\n- `!important`\\n- Overrides through DOM selectors\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe right order is theme → styled → sx; then the styles stay readable and predictable and do not break as the project grows.","const theme = createTheme({\\n  components: {\\n    MuiButton: {\\n      styleOverrides: {\\n        root: {\\n          borderRadius: 8,\\n        },\\n      },\\n    },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n<Button sx={{ mt: 2, backgroundColor: \\"primary.main\\" }} />\\n\\nconst PrimaryButton = styled(Button)({\\n  padding: \\"12px 16px\\",\\n});"],[2226,"A component\'s body in React can be called many times, so placing a request inside it leads to repeated requests on every re-render. That breaks the principle of a pure render and can cause infinite loops. Requests belong to side effects, not to the computation of the UI. They therefore have to be moved into the special mechanisms for effects.","A definition\\nRendering a component is the process of computing the JSX from the props and the state, which ought to be pure and predictable.\\n\\nWhat happens if you make a request in a component\'s body\\nLet us first describe the problem in words.\\n- The component renders\\n- The request is performed\\n- When the state changes a new render happens\\n- The request is performed again\\n\\nWhy this is dangerous\\n\\n- Duplicated requests\\n- Infinite render loops\\n- The difficulty of controlling the lifecycle\\n- Unpredictable behaviour\\n\\nWhy React does not forbid this explicitly\\n\\n- JavaScript technically allows any code to be run\\n- The responsibility for a pure render lies with the developer\\n- React gives you the tools (`useEffect`) for working with side effects properly\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nAny code with side effects (requests, subscriptions, timers) must not sit in a component\'s body.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRequests cannot be made in a component\'s body, because the render has to be pure. React has a separate mechanism for side effects.","function User() {\\n  fetch(\\"/api/user\\"); // ❌ bad\\n  return <div>User</div>;\\n}"],[2227,"`useEffect` is needed to perform side effects such as network requests after the component has rendered. It lets you state explicitly when the effect is to run and when it is to be cleaned up. That gives you control over the request\'s lifecycle and prevents unnecessary calls. Without `useEffect`, code with effects becomes unmanageable.","A definition\\nuseEffect is a React hook for performing side effects after a component has been rendered.\\n\\nHow useEffect solves the problem of requests\\nBefore the list it is important to understand the key idea: the effect runs after the render.\\n- React renders the component\\n- Then it calls `useEffect`\\n- The request is performed inside the effect\\n- When the dependencies change the effect restarts\\n\\nThe role of the array of dependencies\\n\\n- `[]` — run once, on mounting\\n- `[id]` — run when `id` changes\\n- Without the array — run after every render\\n\\nWhy this is safe\\n\\n- The render stays pure\\n- The requests are performed under control\\n- Repeated calls can be managed\\n\\nA practical subtlety\\n`useEffect` does not of itself guarantee that a request is cancelled — for that clean-up logic is needed.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useEffect` is needed in order to isolate side effects from the render and to control the moment at which they run.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetch(\\"/api/user\\");\\n}, []);"],[2228,"A race condition is the situation in which several requests run in parallel and the result of the slower request overwrites the result of the faster one. The application\'s state can end up incorrect as a result. This often happens when the request\'s parameters change quickly. The problem has to do not with the speed of the network but with the order in which the requests finish.","A definition\\nA race condition is a bug arising from the undetermined order in which asynchronous operations finish.\\n\\nA simple example\\nImagine a search-as-you-type:\\n- The user types `\\"r\\"`\\n- The request `search?q=r` is sent\\n- The user quickly types `\\"re\\"`\\n- The request `search?q=re` is sent\\n- The response for `\\"r\\"` arrives later and overwrites the data\\n\\nWhy this is dangerous in React\\n\\n- State is updated asynchronously\\n- React does not know which request is \\"the current one\\"\\n- The UI can show stale data\\n\\nWhere it is encountered most often\\n\\n- Search\\n- Filtering\\n- Switching tabs\\n- Automatic loading of data\\n\\nConclusion\\nA race condition arises when several requests compete to update the state and the order in which they finish is not controlled.",null],[2229,"A race condition in React arises when several asynchronous requests run in parallel and update the same piece of state. React does not know which request is \\"the last\\" or the current one. As a result an older request can finish later and overwrite fresher data. That leads to stale information being displayed in the UI.","A definition\\nA race condition in React is the situation in which the order in which asynchronous operations finish does not coincide with the logical order of the user\'s actions.\\n\\nThe typical scenario in which it arises\\nLet us first look at the most frequent case — requests that depend on props or state.\\n- The component renders with the parameter `query = \\"a\\"`\\n- The request `fetch(\\"a\\")` is sent in `useEffect`\\n- The user quickly changes `query` to `\\"ab\\"`\\n- React performs a new render\\n- `useEffect` sends the request `fetch(\\"ab\\")`\\n- The response for `\\"ab\\"` arrives quickly and updates the state\\n- The response for `\\"a\\"` arrives later and overwrites the state\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nHere:\\n- Each request is independent\\n- React does not control the order in which they finish\\n- The last request to finish \\"wins\\"\\n\\nWhy React cannot solve this itself\\n\\n- React does not know the request\'s business logic\\n- It does not understand which data counts as current\\n- Asynchrony is the developer\'s area of responsibility\\n\\nWhere this is especially critical\\n\\n- Search as you type\\n- Filtering lists\\n- Switching tabs\\n- Components whose props update quickly\\n\\nConclusion\\nA race condition in React arises from the competition of asynchronous requests to update the same piece of state. Without additional control React cannot guarantee that the data is current.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`)\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => {\\n      setResults(data);\\n    });\\n}, [query]);"],[2230,"A cleanup function in `useEffect` lets you cancel or ignore the result of an old request when the effect is no longer current. It is called before the effect runs again or when the component unmounts. Thanks to that, stale requests cannot update the state. It is one of the basic ways of guarding against a race condition.","A definition\\nA cleanup function is a function returned from `useEffect`, which runs when the effect is cleaned up.\\n\\nHow cleanup prevents a race condition\\nLet us first look at the idea at the level of the logic.\\n- The effect starts a request\\n- The dependencies change\\n- React calls the previous effect\'s cleanup\\n- The old request becomes \\"not current\\"\\n- The new effect starts a new request\\n\\nApproach 1: a currency flag\\nA simple, frequently used way.\\n\\nHere:\\n- The old effect is marked as inactive\\n- Its result is ignored\\n- The state is updated only by the current request\\n\\nApproach 2: AbortController\\nThe more \\"correct\\" option for network requests.\\n\\nWhy cleanup is the key mechanism\\n\\n- It is synchronized with the component\'s lifecycle\\n- It works on every change of the dependencies\\n- It lets you manage the effects\' \\"currency\\" explicitly\\n\\nConclusion\\nA cleanup function helps solve a race condition by cancelling or ignoring stale requests. It is an essential tool when working with asynchronous effects in React.","useEffect(() => {\\n  return () => {\\n    // cleanup\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  let isActive = true;\\n\\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`)\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => {\\n      if (isActive) {\\n        setResults(data);\\n      }\\n    });\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    isActive = false;\\n  };\\n}, [query]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n\\n  fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`, {\\n    signal: controller.signal\\n  })\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => {\\n      setResults(data);\\n    })\\n    .catch(err => {\\n      if (err.name !== \\"AbortError\\") {\\n        throw err;\\n      }\\n    });\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    controller.abort();\\n  };\\n}, [query]);"],[2231,"Debounce lets you defer sending a request until the user has finished typing or changing the parameters. That reduces the number of requests and lowers the likelihood of a race condition. Instead of a great many competing requests, a single one — the most current — is sent. In React debounce is often used together with `useEffect`.","The basic idea\\nBefore we look at it, it is important to understand that debounce does not \\"fix\\" a race condition directly, but it reduces the likelihood of one arising considerably.\\n\\nHow debounce affects requests\\n\\n- The user changes a value quickly\\n- The debounce timer is constantly reset\\n- The request is sent only after a pause\\n- The number of simultaneous requests drops sharply\\n\\nAn example with debounce inside an effect\\n\\nWhy cleanup matters here\\n\\n- `clearTimeout` cancels the previous \\"scheduled\\" request\\n- The old effects do not survive to be executed\\n- Only current data ends up in the state\\n\\nThe link between debounce and race conditions\\n\\n- Fewer requests — less competition\\n- Less chance that an old request will arrive after a new one\\n- The UX becomes more stable and more predictable\\n\\nThe practical approach\\nIn practice a combination is often used:\\n- Debounce — to reduce the number of requests\\n- Cleanup / AbortController — to guarantee correctness\\n\\nConclusion\\nDebounce helps optimize repeated requests and reduces the likelihood of a race condition, especially in scenarios where data is typed in frequently. Combined with cleanup it gives a reliable, scalable solution.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const handler = setTimeout(() => {\\n    fetch(`/api/search?q=${query}`)\\n      .then(res => res.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setResults(data);\\n      });\\n  }, 300);\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    clearTimeout(handler);\\n  };\\n}, [query]);"],[2232,"In React 18 in development mode `useEffect` can be called twice because of `StrictMode`. That is done deliberately, in order to reveal unsafe side effects. In production there is no such behaviour. The two calls help you find code that is not ready to be run again.","A definition\\nStrictMode is a React mode that helps you find potential bugs by deliberately running some operations again.\\n\\nWhat exactly happens\\nBefore the list it is important to understand that this is not a bug but diagnostic behaviour.\\n- React mounts the component\\n- It calls `useEffect`\\n- It calls the cleanup\\n- It mounts the component once more\\n- It calls `useEffect` again\\n\\nIn development mode the log will be:\\n- `\\"effect\\"`\\n- `\\"cleanup\\"`\\n- `\\"effect\\"`\\n\\nWhy React does this\\n\\n- It checks whether the cleanup works properly\\n- It reveals:\\n- subscriptions that were not cancelled\\n- duplicated requests\\n- unsafe side effects\\n\\n- It makes you write resilient code\\n\\nWhy this matters for requests\\nIf a request:\\n- Is made without cleanup\\n- Is not cancelled\\n- Is not protected against a race condition\\n\\n— you will see a double request in dev mode.\\n\\nHow to work with this\\n\\n- Always use cleanup\\n- Do not rely on an effect running \\"only once\\"\\n- Understand the difference between dev and prod\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe double call of `useEffect` in React is a consequence of `StrictMode`. It helps you find bugs in your handling of side effects and makes your code more reliable.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"effect\\");\\n  return () => {\\n    console.log(\\"cleanup\\");\\n  };\\n}, []);"],[2233,"Cancelling a request aborts the network request itself, whereas ignoring the result simply stops a stale response updating the state. Cancellation saves resources and bandwidth, while ignoring is simpler to implement. Both approaches guard against a race condition, but they solve the problem at different levels. They are often used together.","The main difference\\nBefore we look at it, it is important to separate these two approaches logically.\\n\\n1. Ignoring the result\\nThe request is performed in full, but its result counts as not current.\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- The request goes out all the same\\n- The response arrives\\n- The state is not updated if the effect is stale\\n\\n2. Cancelling the request\\nThe request is physically aborted.\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- The request is stopped\\n- The server may not even process it fully\\n- Resources are saved\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\n\\n- A simple UI → ignoring the result\\n- Frequent requests → cancellation through an `AbortController`\\n- Critical scenarios → both approaches together\\n\\nConclusion\\nIgnoring the result and cancelling the request solve the same problem, but at different levels. The best solution is to choose the approach deliberately for the particular scenario.","useEffect(() => {\\n  let active = true;\\n\\n  fetch(\\"/api/data\\")\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => {\\n      if (active) {\\n        setData(data);\\n      }\\n    });\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    active = false;\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n\\n  fetch(\\"/api/data\\", { signal: controller.signal })\\n    .then(res => res.json())\\n    .then(data => setData(data))\\n    .catch(err => {\\n      if (err.name !== \\"AbortError\\") {\\n        throw err;\\n      }\\n    });\\n\\n  return () => controller.abort();\\n}, []);"],[2234,"Most often people forget to add dependencies, which leads to stale data, or else they add unnecessary ones, which causes unnecessary effects. The mistake with functions and objects that are recreated on every render is also widespread. All of that can lead to race conditions and to bugs that are hard to debug. Working with dependencies properly is one of a React developer\'s key skills.","The basic idea\\n`useEffect` always works with the values that were available at the moment it was created.\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n1. Omitted dependencies\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- A stale value is used\\n- The effect does not respond to changes\\n- The behaviour becomes non-obvious\\n\\n2. Unnecessary dependencies\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- The effect runs too often\\n- Unnecessary requests are possible\\n\\n3. Objects and arrays in the dependencies\\n\\nIf `filters` is created anew:\\n- React considers it changed\\n- The effect restarts\\n\\nHow to work with this\\n\\n- Use `useCallback` and `useMemo`\\n- Follow the recommendations of `eslint-plugin-react-hooks`\\n- Understand which values really ought to be in the dependencies\\n\\nConclusion\\nMistakes in `useEffect`\'s dependencies are one of the most frequent causes of bugs in React. Understanding closures and dependencies directly affects the stability of asynchronous code.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetch(`/api/user/${id}`);\\n}, []); // ❌ id is used but not listed\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData();\\n}, [fetchData]); // fetchData is recreated on every render\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData(filters);\\n}, [filters]);"],[2235,"Changing `state` leads to the component re-rendering, whereas changing a `ref` does not. `state` is used for data that affects the display of the UI. A `ref` is for holding mutable values that have to be preserved between renders but take no part in the painting. React does not track changes to `ref.current`.","A definition\\n\\n- `state` — a component\'s reactive state, a change to which initiates a re-render\\n- `ref` — a container for a mutable value, a change to which does not affect the render\\n\\nChanging `state`\\n\\n- `setState` / `setX` is called\\n- React schedules a re-render\\n- The component repaints with the new state\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nChanging a `ref`\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- `state`\\n- data for the UI\\n- the result of a request\\n- display flags\\n\\n- `ref`\\n- timers\\n- previous values\\n- references to the DOM\\n- counters with no UI\\n\\nConclusion\\nIf the data has to affect the interface, use `state`. If you need to keep a value between renders without a repaint, use a `ref`.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nsetCount(count + 1); // it causes a re-render\\n\\nconst countRef = useRef(0);\\n\\ncountRef.current += 1; // there will be no render"],[2236,"When the `state` changes React performs a repeated render of the component. The UI will display the state\'s value as it is for the current render. React will compare the new virtual tree against the previous one and update only the parts of the DOM that have changed. The user will see the updated interface.","What happens on `setState`\\n\\n- The state update goes into the queue\\n- React starts a new render\\n- The JSX is computed again\\n- React applies the minimal changes to the DOM\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nAfter the click:\\n- the component re-renders\\n- the `<p>` displays `\\"B\\"`\\n\\nAn important subtlety\\n\\n- `setState` is asynchronous\\n- Within one render a single consistent state is always used\\n- The old values are not available in the new JSX\\n\\nA frequent mistake\\n\\n- the final `count` will increase by `1`, not by `2`\\n\\nConclusion\\nA component always displays the state of a particular render, not \\"partly updated\\" values.","const [text, setText] = useState(\'A\');\\n\\n<button onClick={() => setText(\'B\')} />\\n<p>{text}</p>\\n\\nsetCount(count + 1);\\nsetCount(count + 1);"],[2237,"A `force remount` is the forced recreation of a component from scratch rather than an update to it. Most often it is done by changing the `key`. On a remount the component loses all of its internal state and its effects run again. It is used rarely and deliberately.","What a remount is\\nA remount is the situation in which React:\\n- unmounts the component\\n- creates a new instance\\n- runs `useEffect` again\\n\\nHow to cause a `force remount`\\nThe most widespread way is the `key`:\\n\\nWhen `id` changes:\\n- the old component is removed\\n- a new one mounts afresh\\n\\nWhat happens on a remount\\n\\n- The `state` is reset\\n- The `ref` is recreated\\n- The effects run again\\n- The DOM nodes are created afresh\\n\\nWhen this is justified\\n\\n- Resetting complex internal state\\n- Fully reloading a form\\n- Recreating a third-party widget\\n- Isolating components with independent lifecycles\\n\\nWhen it should not be used\\n\\n- For ordinary data updates\\n- Instead of working with `state` properly\\n- As a \\"crutch\\" without understanding the causes\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `force remount` is a powerful but dangerous tool. It is used only when a complete reset of the component is needed rather than simply an update to the data.","<Component key={id} />"],[2238,"Unsubscribing is done through the cleanup function that `useEffect` returns. That function is called before the effect runs again and when the component unmounts. In the cleanup, subscriptions, timers, event listeners and requests are normally cancelled. Without unsubscribing properly, memory leaks and errors from updating the state of an unmounted component are possible.","A definition\\nA cleanup function is a function returned from `useEffect`, which is used to clean side effects up.\\n\\nWhen the cleanup is called\\n\\n- Before the effect\'s next run (if the dependencies have changed)\\n- When the component unmounts\\n\\nA basic example with a subscription\\n\\nSubscriptions to external sources\\n\\nThe frequent mistakes\\n\\n- Forgetting to return a cleanup function\\n- Using new function references when unsubscribing\\n- Updating the `state` after the unmount\\n\\nAsynchronous effects\\nFor requests:\\n- an `AbortController` is used\\n- or an \\"isMounted\\" flag (less preferable)\\n\\nConclusion\\nAny effect that subscribes to or starts something has to be cleaned up symmetrically. Cleanup is an essential part of a correct `useEffect`.","useEffect(() => {\\n  const handler = () => console.log(\'resize\');\\n\\n  window.addEventListener(\'resize\', handler);\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    window.removeEventListener(\'resize\', handler);\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const unsubscribe = store.subscribe(() => {\\n    // the update\\n  });\\n\\n  return () => {\\n    unsubscribe();\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const controller = new AbortController();\\n\\n  fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });\\n\\n  return () => controller.abort();\\n}, [url]);"],[2239,"`state` causes a re-render and is used for the UI. A `ref` keeps a value between renders but causes no update to the interface. Local variables are recreated on every render and keep no state. Each tool solves its own problem and they are not interchangeable.","A definition and the key idea\\nIn React there are three fundamentally different ways of holding data, and the choice affects the component\'s behaviour.\\n\\n`state`\\n\\n- Reactive\\n- Held between renders\\n- A change → a re-render\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n- data that is displayed\\n- UI logic\\n- a form\'s state\\n\\n`ref`\\n\\n- Mutable\\n- Held between renders\\n- A change causes no re-render\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n- DOM elements\\n- timers\\n- previous values\\n- flags that do not affect the UI\\n\\nLocal variables\\n\\n- They are created anew on every render\\n- They keep no state\\n- They are suited only to computations\\n\\nThe key differences in behaviour\\n\\n- `state` — the source of truth for the UI\\n- `ref` — a store \\"between renders\\"\\n- local variables — one-off\\n\\nConclusion\\nIf the data has to live between renders and affect the UI — `state`.\\nIf it has to live but not affect the UI — a `ref`.\\nIf it is needed only for the current computation — a local variable.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nconst prevValue = useRef(null);\\n\\nconst doubled = count * 2;"],[2240,"A `ref` is used to hold data that changes over time but should not cause a re-render. That lets you avoid unnecessary UI updates and improve performance. A `ref` keeps its value between renders and is available synchronously. React ignores changes to `ref.current`.","A definition\\n`useRef` is a hook that returns an object with a `current` field, intended for holding mutable values.\\n\\nWhy not `state`\\nIf you use `state`:\\n- every change will cause a re-render\\n- the UI will update unnecessarily\\n- performance problems are possible\\n\\nThe typical cases for a `ref`\\n\\n- Timers and intervals\\n- Event counters\\n- The previous value of a prop or of state\\n- Lifecycle flags\\n\\nAn example: the previous value\\n\\nAn example: a timer\\n\\nAn important subtlety\\n\\n- Changing `ref.current` does not initiate a re-render\\n- The value is available immediately\\n- A `ref` lives as long as the component does\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `ref` is a tool for managing mutable state outside the render. It is needed when performance and control matter rather than the display of data.","const prevCount = useRef(count);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  prevCount.current = count;\\n}, [count]);\\n\\nconst timerId = useRef(null);\\n\\ntimerId.current = setTimeout(() => {\\n  // the logic\\n}, 1000);"],[2241,"Frontend architecture is normally built around a division by layer or by feature. The feature-based approach, where each feature is isolated, is often used. The shared parts are moved into a shared layer. The architecture should make maintenance, testing and scaling easier.","The basic idea\\nArchitecture answers the question of where the code sits and why, not merely of how it works.\\n\\nThe popular approaches\\n\\n1) Layer-based (by layer)\\n\\n- `components`\\n- `services`\\n- `store`\\n- `api`\\n\\nThe cons:\\n- it scales badly\\n- strong coupling\\n\\n2) Feature-based (by feature)\\nThe more modern and more popular approach.\\n\\nAn example of the structure:\\n\\nThe pros:\\n- Isolation of the logic\\n- Features are easier to remove and to add\\n- It scales better\\n\\nThe layers used most often\\n\\n- UI (components)\\n- Business logic\\n- Data / API\\n- State management\\n\\nThe key principles\\n\\n- Minimal coupling\\n- Explicit dependencies\\n- Reusable shared modules\\n- A transparent data flow\\n\\nConclusion\\nGood frontend architecture is not an \\"ideal scheme\\" but a structure that grows together with the project while staying clear and manageable.","features/\\n  auth/\\n    components/\\n    api/\\n    model/\\n  profile/\\nshared/\\n  ui/\\n  hooks/\\n  utils/"],[2242,"A re-render leads to the component\'s function being run again and to the Virtual DOM being recomputed. With a large number of components that can be expensive in time terms. The additional comparisons and DOM updates also affect performance. It is therefore important to minimize unnecessary re-renders.","Every update to the state or the props can start a re-render.\\n\\nWhat happens on a re-render\\n\\n- A repeated call of the component\'s function\\n- The creation of a new Virtual DOM\\n- A comparison against the previous version\\n- An update to the real DOM where necessary\\n\\nWhy this is expensive\\n\\n- A large component tree\\n- Frequent state updates\\n- Complex render logic\\n\\nHow to reduce the cost\\n\\n- `React.memo`\\n- `useCallback`\\n- `useMemo`\\n\\nConclusion\\nControlling re-renders is the key to the performance of React applications.",null],[2243,"The component-based approach lets you break an interface down into independent parts. Each component is responsible for its own logic and its own display. That makes reusing code and maintaining an application easier. The approach makes an interface more predictable.","Components are the foundation of React\'s architecture.\\n\\nWhat a component is\\nA component is an independent block of an interface with its own logic and its own state.\\n\\nThe advantages of the component-based approach\\n\\n- Reuse\\n- Isolation of the logic\\n- Ease of testing\\n- Easier refactoring\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe component-based approach makes React applications scalable and convenient for team development.","function Button({ label }) {\\n  return <button>{label}</button>;\\n}"],[2244,"To optimize a search over large arrays of data you can use several approaches:\\n- Make the search asynchronous, using Web Workers or setTimeout.\\n- Break the data into pages (pagination) and load only the part you need.\\n- Use search algorithms of lower complexity, such as a binary search for sorted data.","When the data is too large and we try to process all of it at once on the client, that can lead to a considerable load on the browser, especially on mobile devices. Here are several ways of solving the problem:\\n- An asynchronous search:\\n- The use of `Web Workers` lets you perform the search on a background thread without blocking the main UI thread.\\n- For smaller volumes you can use `setTimeout`, in order to divide the search into parts and update the UI gradually.\\n\\n- Paginating the data:\\n- Instead of loading all of the data at once, you can break it into pages. The user will work with only a small part of the data.\\n- That not only speeds the search up but reduces the load on the browser considerably.\\n\\n- The use of efficient search algorithms:\\n- For sorted data you can use a binary search, which has a complexity of O(log n) rather than the O(n) of a linear search.\\n\\nConclusion: for large arrays of data it is important to optimize both the search and the loading of the data. Pagination, asynchronous approaches and efficient algorithms can improve performance considerably.",null],[2245,"To optimize working with nested objects you can:\\n- Use memoization in order to store the intermediate results.\\n- Apply lazy-loading techniques, so as to load only the parts you need.\\n- Use a `Map` or a `Set` for an efficient search.","Working with large nested objects can be expensive in performance terms, especially if the data has to be changed or inspected often. Let us look at several approaches to optimization:\\n- Memoization:\\n- Memoization lets you store the results of computations and reuse them, which reduces the execution time. It is applied to functions that are called often with the same parameters.\\n- An example:\\n\\n- Lazy loading:\\n- You can load the data as it is needed rather than loading the whole object at once. This is especially useful when working with data that may not always be needed.\\n\\n- The use of `Map` and `Set`:\\n- When you have to work with large volumes of data and search for or update elements often, it is better to use data structures such as a `Map` or a `Set`, which give a fast search and insertion.\\n\\nConclusion: to optimize working with large nested objects it is important to avoid unnecessary computation and to load only the data you need. Memoization and lazy approaches help improve performance substantially.","const memoize = (fn) => {\\n  const cache = new Map();\\n  return (...args) => {\\n    const key = JSON.stringify(args);\\n    if (!cache.has(key)) {\\n      cache.set(key, fn(...args));\\n    }\\n    return cache.get(key);\\n  };\\n};"],[2246,"To improve the readability of code in loops you can use:\\n- The decomposition of complex operations into separate functions.\\n- Local variables for the intermediate results.\\n- Array methods such as `map`, `filter` and `reduce` instead of ordinary loops.","When you work with large, complex data structures, loops can become hard to take in. Here are several ways of improving the readability of the code:\\n- Decomposition:\\n- Break the complex operations performed inside the loop into separate functions. That not only improves readability but helps you reuse the code.\\n- An example:\\n\\n- Local variables:\\n- Use clearly named variables to hold the intermediate results, which helps you avoid confusion and makes the code more readable.\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The use of array methods:\\n- Instead of standard loops you can use array methods such as `map`, `filter` and `reduce`, which make the code more declarative and more readable.\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion: to improve the readability of code in loops it is important to use the principles of decomposition and clear variable names, and to apply the built-in array methods, in order to make the code clearer and more maintainable.","// The bad version\\nfor (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n  if (data[i].isActive) {\\n    processItem(data[i]);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The good version\\nconst processActiveItems = (item) => {\\n  if (item.isActive) {\\n    processItem(item);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n  processActiveItems(data[i]);\\n}\\n\\n// A local variable for holding the intermediate values\\nconst result = processData(item);\\nif (result.isValid) {\\n  doSomething(result);\\n}\\n\\nconst activeItems = data.filter(item => item.isActive);\\nactiveItems.forEach(item => processItem(item));"],[2247,"A Canvas does not hold its state in a declarative form, so that state has to be moved into external state. The UI layers panel works with that state, while the Canvas merely reflects it. Any changes have to go through a single source of data. The Canvas and the UI subscribe to one and the same state.","Canvas is an imperative API, whereas the UI in React is declarative. That is the key contradiction that has to be resolved architecturally.\\n\\nThe basic principle\\nThe scene\'s state should be:\\n- outside the Canvas\\n- serializable\\n- shared by the UI and the Canvas\\n\\nThe Canvas is not the source of truth.\\n\\nThe typical architecture\\n\\n- A single scene state\\n- an array of layers\\n- the objects\' properties\\n- the painting order\\n\\n- The UI layers panel\\n- it reads the state\\n- it initiates changes (visibility, order, select)\\n\\n- A Canvas adapter\\n- it listens for changes in the state\\n- it applies them to the Canvas API\\n\\nThe data flow\\n\\n- UI → state → Canvas\\n- Canvas events → state → UI\\n\\nA practical example of the logic\\n\\nThe Canvas meanwhile:\\n- receives the new state\\n- repaints the scene or the object\\n\\nIn short\\nThe Canvas must not be the source of the state. The synchronization is achieved through external state, which governs both the UI and the painting, which gives predictability and scalability.","// changing a layer\'s visibility\\nsetLayers(prev => updateVisibility(prev, id))"],[2248,"Nested drag-and-drop is built around the hierarchy of the data rather than of the DOM. When something is dragged, the data structure changes and the UI merely reflects those changes. To implement it, libraries or logic of your own with the computation of the target positions are used. The key difficulty is updating the nested nodes correctly.","Drag-and-drop in React is always work with state rather than with direct DOM manipulation.\\n\\nThe key idea\\nWhat is dragged is not an interface element but:\\n- a node\'s identifier\\n- its position in the tree\\n\\nThe UI is a projection of the data structure.\\n\\nThe main steps of the implementation\\n\\n- Representing the data\\n- a tree with an `id` and `children`\\n- explicit references to the parents\\n\\n- Handling the drag events\\n- determining the source\\n- determining the target\\n- checking whether the nesting is permissible\\n\\n- Updating the structure\\n- removing the node from its old place\\n- inserting it in the new place\\n\\nAn example of the data-change logic\\n\\nThe typical difficulties\\n\\n- preventing a node being nested inside itself\\n- computing the indexes correctly\\n- performance with large trees\\n\\nIn short\\nNested drag-and-drop is a problem of managing a tree of data. If the structure is updated correctly, React will paint the right UI itself without any manual manipulation.","// removeNode(tree, sourceId)\\n// insertNode(tree, targetId, node)"],[2249,"The main causes of freezes are unnecessary re-renders, heavy logic in the render and large amounts of DOM. The problems often arise from working with state and props incorrectly. Synchronous operations and unoptimized effects also play a part. Most lag is solved by a proper architecture and memoization.","Freezes in React are almost always related not to React itself but to how its model is used.\\n\\nThe main causes\\nBefore optimizing, it is important to identify the source of the problem.\\n- Unnecessary re-renders\\n- the state is updated when it need not be\\n- changes are \\"drilled\\" too deep\\n\\n- Heavy logic in the render\\n- computations inside the component\\n- sorting and filtering without memoization\\n\\n- Large lists\\n- thousands of items without virtualization\\n- frequent DOM updates\\n\\n- Synchronous blocking operations\\n- complex loops\\n- parsing large amounts of data\\n\\nThe typical solutions\\n\\n- `React.memo`\\n- `useMemo`, `useCallback`\\n- virtualizing lists\\n- moving logic out of the render\\n\\nIn short\\nFreezes in React are a consequence of managing state and renders incorrectly. Optimization begins with reducing the amount of work React does on every update.",null],[2250,"When the parent\'s state changes React calls its render function again. Every child component repaints too, because React does not know whether their data has changed. This is React\'s basic behaviour. Optimization is possible through memoization.","A re-render in React is not an update to the DOM but a recomputation of the UI.\\n\\nHow the update works\\nWhen the state changes:\\n- React calls the parent\'s render\\n- A new virtual tree is created\\n- The child components are called again\\n\\nThat happens even if their props have stayed the same.\\n\\nWhy React does this\\n\\n- a simple, predictable model\\n- the absence of deep comparisons by default\\n- a high diffing speed\\n\\nHow to avoid unnecessary re-renders\\n\\n- `React.memo` for functional components\\n- `PureComponent` for classes\\n- splitting the state up\\n- moving the unchanging parts of the tree out\\n\\nIn short\\nThe re-rendering of child components is normal React behaviour. Optimization is achieved not by prohibitions but by dividing the state properly and by memoization.",null],[2251,"`React.memo` is a wrapper for a functional component that skips a re-render if the props have not changed. `PureComponent` is a base class for a class component that does the same thing but also compares the `state`. In both cases a shallow comparison is used. If the props or the state change by reference, the component will count as having changed.","`React.memo` and `PureComponent` solve the same problem: reducing the number of unnecessary re-renders. They are used in different paradigms, however, and have different \\"areas of responsibility\\".\\n\\nWhat exactly they optimize\\nBoth tools add a check of \\"do I need to re-render\\".\\n- `React.memo(Component)`\\n- it works only with props\\n- it is applicable only to functional components\\n- by default it performs a shallow compare of the props\\n\\n- `class X extends React.PureComponent`\\n- it compares both the props and the state\\n- it is applicable only to class components\\n- it also uses a shallow compare\\n\\nImportant: a shallow compare means React compares the references of objects/arrays rather than their \\"insides\\".\\n\\nWhy this matters in practice\\nLet us look at the typical reason why an optimization \\"does not take effect\\": every render creates new references.\\n\\nEven if the contents are the same, `options` is always a new object, and `React.memo` will see that \\"the props have changed\\".\\n\\nHow to apply it properly\\n\\n- Stabilize the references (where that is justified)\\n- `useMemo` for objects/arrays\\n- `useCallback` for functions\\n\\n- Choose where to optimize\\n- optimize only the \\"expensive\\" components\\n- do not blanket everything in memoization\\n\\nA custom comparison in React.memo\\n`React.memo` lets you pass a comparison function of your own:\\n\\nBut it is a tool with risks:\\n- you can make a mistake and end up with a stale UI\\n- the comparison can be more expensive than the render itself\\n\\nThe differences in behaviour and in limitations\\n\\n- The scope of the comparison\\n- `PureComponent`: props + state\\n- `React.memo`: props only (state inside the component still triggers a re-render)\\n\\n- The type of component\\n- `PureComponent`: classes\\n- `React.memo`: functions\\n\\n- Manageability\\n- `React.memo` combines more easily with hooks and modern React\\n- `PureComponent` is historically important, but new projects are more often built on functions\\n\\nHow this relates to \\"why the children re-render\\"\\nIf the parent has re-rendered, then by default its children will be called again too.\\n`React.memo` / `PureComponent` let you \\"stop the wave\\" of re-renders, but only if:\\n- the props really have not changed (by shallow compare)\\n- you are not creating new objects/functions on every render without memoization\\n\\nIn short\\n`React.memo` and `PureComponent` are the same principle of optimization but for different types of component. They are effective when the props/state are stable by reference and when the optimization is applied selectively to components that really are expensive.","const Child = React.memo(({ options }) => {\\n  // ...\\n})\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const options = { mode: \'A\' } // a new reference on every render\\n  return <Child options={options} />\\n}\\n\\nconst options = useMemo(() => ({ mode: \'A\' }), [])\\n\\nReact.memo(Component, (prev, next) => {\\n  // true => skip the re-render\\n  return prev.id === next.id\\n})"],[2252,"Memoizing values (`useMemo`) stores the result of a computation, so that it is not recomputed unnecessarily. Memoizing functions (`useCallback`) stores the reference to a function, so that it is not created anew. In React this matters because of the shallow comparison of props and dependencies. Both tools are useful when the computations are expensive or when the reference affects the re-rendering of child components.","React relies on referential identity: if an object/function is new by reference, that counts as a change. Hence unnecessary re-renders and unnecessary recomputation.\\n\\nMemoizing values (useMemo)\\n`useMemo` remembers a \\"value\\", normally:\\n- the result of a computation\\n- an object/array whose reference it matters to preserve\\n\\nWhen it is useful:\\n- Expensive computations\\n- sorting\\n- filtering\\n- aggregations\\n\\n- Stabilizing props\\n- so that a child\'s `React.memo` takes effect\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nMemoizing functions (useCallback)\\n`useCallback` remembers \\"the reference to a function\\".\\n\\nThis matters because:\\n- a function declared in a component is created anew on every render\\n- if it is passed down as a prop, `React.memo` will see that \\"the props have changed\\"\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhat the key difference is\\n\\n- `useMemo` returns a value\\n- `useCallback` returns a function\\n\\nIn meaning, `useCallback(fn, deps)` is equivalent to:\\n\\nBut `useCallback` reads more easily when functions are precisely what is at issue.\\n\\nWhen memoization is not needed (and can be harmful)\\nMemoization is not a free optimization.\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- the code becomes more complicated\\n- the risk of mistakes in the dependencies\\n- the overhead of comparing the deps\\n\\nIt is therefore worth applying when there is a clear reason:\\n- the component really is expensive\\n- the function/object really does break the children\'s memoization\\n- the computation really is heavy\\n\\nThe practical link with React.memo\\nIf you have a child wrapped in `React.memo` and you pass into it:\\n- an object\\n- an array\\n- a function\\n\\nthen without `useMemo` / `useCallback` it will often turn out that the child repaints all the same, because of the new references.\\n\\nIn short\\nMemoizing values reduces the amount of recomputation and stabilizes the references to data, while memoizing functions stabilizes the references to handlers. Both tools help `React.memo` and effect dependencies work predictably, but they have to be applied selectively.","const filtered = useMemo(() => {\\n  return items.filter(x => x.includes(query))\\n}, [items, query])\\n\\nconst onSelect = useCallback((id) => {\\n  setSelectedId(id)\\n}, [])\\n\\nuseMemo(() => fn, deps)"],[2253,"A single source of truth is the principle that data has one authoritative source. Every part of the application reads the data from it and changes it through agreed mechanisms. This makes the logic simpler and prevents the state getting out of sync. The principle is widely used in React and in state managers.","A single source of truth is not a particular library but an architectural idea.\\n\\nWhat the principle consists in\\nEach logical set of data:\\n- has one place where it is held\\n- has no duplicate copies carrying \\"partial truth\\"\\n- is changed through a predictable flow\\n\\nThat reduces the number of bugs and makes maintenance easier.\\n\\nAn example of the problem without this principle\\nIf the same piece of state:\\n- is held in the parent\\n- is copied into the child component\\n- is partly duplicated in local state\\n\\nquestions arise:\\n- which value is current\\n- who ought to update the data\\n- why the UI has got out of sync\\n\\nWhat this looks like in React\\nThe typical single sources:\\n- the parent component\'s state\\n- a global store (Redux, Zustand)\\n- the URL (query params, router state)\\n\\nThe components:\\n- receive the data through props or hooks\\n- do not own copies of that data\\n\\nThe link with predictability\\nA single source of truth:\\n- makes debugging easier\\n- makes the application\'s behaviour deterministic\\n- makes testing easier\\n\\nWhen the principle is broken deliberately\\nSometimes exceptions are acceptable:\\n- the temporary local state of an input\\n- UI state (hover, focus)\\n- optimistic updates\\n\\nWhat matters is that these be deliberate decisions rather than accidental duplication.\\n\\nIn short\\nA single source of truth is the principle that makes an application\'s state clear and manageable. It reduces the number of bugs and is the foundation of the architecture of React applications and of state managers.",null],[2254,"Atoms are the minimal independent units of state. Each atom holds a small piece of data and is updated separately. Components subscribe only to the atoms they need. That reduces the number of unnecessary re-renders and makes scaling state easier.","Atomic state appeared as a reaction to the problems of large centralized stores.\\n\\nThe problems with classic stores\\nWith a single large state object:\\n- updates touch many subscribers\\n- it is hard to localize the changes\\n- the coupling between components grows\\n\\nEven a small change can lead to a wide wave of re-renders.\\n\\nThe idea of atoms\\nAn atom is:\\n- the minimal part of the state\\n- an independent source of data\\n- the unit of subscription for components\\n\\nA component:\\n- subscribes only to the atoms it needs\\n- does not respond to changes in the rest of the state\\n\\nHow this affects re-renders\\n\\n- one atom changed → only the dependent components updated\\n- there is no need to compare a large state object\\n- the \\"noise\\" of updates is reduced\\n\\nThe additional capabilities\\nModern atom-based approaches often support:\\n- computed atoms (derived state)\\n- asynchronous atoms\\n- the dynamic creation of state\\n\\nThat makes the model flexible and modular.\\n\\nThe architectural consequences\\n\\n- the state comes closer to the components\\n- the global coupling is reduced\\n- functionality is easier to remove and to add\\n\\nIn short\\nAtomic state breaks the global state into independent parts, which makes the updates more targeted and more predictable. That makes scaling easier and improves the performance of complex React applications.",null],[2255,"Signals let you update only the parts of the UI that really depend on the data that has changed. That reduces the number of re-renders and improves performance. The model becomes less declarative and harder to debug, however. Signals call for careful architectural application.","Signals are an alternative model of state management, based on targeted reactivity.\\n\\nThe essence of the approach\\nA signal:\\n- holds a value\\n- knows who uses it\\n- notifies only those consumers when it changes\\n\\nUnlike React state:\\n- it does not initiate a re-render of the whole component\\n- it updates only the particular dependencies\\n\\nThe advantages of signals\\n\\n- Fine-grained updates\\n- only the dependent part of the UI is updated\\n- less unnecessary work\\n\\n- Performance\\n- it is especially noticeable in large trees\\n- it suits frequent updates well\\n\\n- A simple data model\\n- less memoization\\n- fewer dependencies and `useMemo`s\\n\\nThe disadvantages and the risks\\n\\n- The complexity of the mental model\\n- the dependencies are not always obvious\\n- the source of an update is harder to trace\\n\\n- Integration with React\\n- it does not coincide entirely with React\'s philosophy\\n- it requires adapters and wrappers\\n\\n- Potential bugs\\n- hidden dependencies\\n- unexpected UI updates\\n\\nWhen signals are justified\\n\\n- complex, dynamic interfaces\\n- a large number of small updates\\n- scenarios where re-renders become the bottleneck\\n\\nIn short\\nSignals give high performance thanks to their targeted updates, but they complicate the architecture and the debugging. They are a powerful tool that is worth applying deliberately rather than by default.",null],[2256,"Different state managers solve different problems and are optimized for different scenarios. They have different priorities: simplicity, performance, predictability, scalability. There is no universal solution, because projects\' requirements differ greatly. The ecosystem therefore stays diverse.","State managers are a reflection of different architectural approaches rather than a competition for \\"the one right solution\\".\\n\\nWhy a single solution is not enough\\nApplications differ in:\\n- size\\n- the complexity of their logic\\n- how often they update\\n- their performance requirements\\n\\nWhat is good for one project can be excessive or awkward for another.\\n\\nThe different focuses of the solutions\\nDifferent approaches have different strengths:\\n- Centralized stores\\n- a predictable data flow\\n- convenient for large teams\\n\\n- Lightweight stores\\n- minimal boilerplate\\n- fast to introduce\\n\\n- Atom-based solutions\\n- targeted updates\\n- good scalability in terms of renders\\n\\n- Signal-based approaches\\n- maximum reactivity\\n- harder to understand\\n\\nWhy a \\"perfect\\" manager is impossible\\nEvery state manager makes a choice:\\n- between simplicity and control\\n- between declarativeness and performance\\n- between explicitness and magic\\n\\nOptimizing everything at once is impossible.\\n\\nHow to choose properly\\nThe right question is not \\"which is better\\" but:\\n- what problems there are now\\n- what will appear as the project grows\\n- how much complexity the team is prepared to maintain\\n\\nIn short\\nThe diversity of state managers exists because projects\' tasks and constraints differ. The best choice is the one that solves the current problems without excessive complexity.",null],[2257,"List virtualization is the technique in which only the visible items are rendered into the DOM. The other items are not created until they come into the visible area. That reduces the load on the browser and on React sharply. As a result the interface stays smooth even with thousands of items.","Rendering a large number of DOM elements is one of the most frequent causes of performance problems.\\n\\nWhat the problem with ordinary lists is\\nIf you render a list of thousands of items:\\n- the size of the DOM grows\\n- the layout and paint time increases\\n- every re-render becomes expensive\\n\\nEven if the user sees only 20 items, the browser processes all of them.\\n\\nThe idea of virtualization\\nVirtualization is built on a simple idea:\\n- the user sees only part of the list\\n- so only that part needs rendering\\n\\nThe rest:\\n- is replaced by empty spacers\\n- is loaded as the user scrolls\\n\\nHow it works conceptually\\n\\n- The height of the items is computed\\n- The current visible zone is determined\\n- Only the items inside it are rendered\\n- As the user scrolls, the set of items changes\\n\\nWhat this gives you\\n\\n- a sharp reduction in the number of DOM nodes\\n- fast re-renders\\n- smooth scrolling\\n\\nWhen virtualization is essential\\n\\n- lists of hundreds and thousands of items\\n- tables\\n- feeds with dynamic data\\n\\nIn short\\nVirtualization lets you render large lists without losing performance, by limiting the number of DOM elements to the visible area alone. It is a basic optimization technique for large React applications.",null],[2258,"In React ready-made virtualization libraries are the usual choice. They take on the computation of the visible area, the management of the scrolling and the re-rendering of the items. The most popular solutions are `react-window` and `react-virtualized`. The choice depends on the complexity of the requirements and on how much functionality is needed.","Virtualization is a widespread task, so stable solutions have formed in the React ecosystem.\\n\\nThe main libraries\\n\\n- react-window\\n- minimalist and lightweight\\n- it supports lists and grids\\n- a simple API and high performance\\n\\nIt is the most common choice when:\\n- the items are of the same kind\\n- the row sizes are known\\n- no complex logic is needed\\n\\n- react-virtualized\\n- heavier and more featureful\\n- support for tables, auto-sizing and windows\\n- more abstractions and settings\\n\\nIt is suited to:\\n- complex tables\\n- items of varying sizes\\n- advanced scenarios\\n\\n- react-virtuoso\\n- oriented towards UX\\n- it works well with dynamic heights\\n- fewer manual computations\\n\\nIt is often chosen for:\\n- feeds\\n- chats\\n- infinite lists\\n\\nThe libraries\' common features\\nThey all:\\n- render only the visible items\\n- use a container with scrolling\\n- recompute the range as the user scrolls\\n\\nThey differ in:\\n- the flexibility of their API\\n- how hard they are to introduce\\n- their bundle size\\n\\nIn short\\nFor most tasks `react-window` is enough. More complex scenarios call for `react-virtualized` or `react-virtuoso`. Using a library is almost always preferable to an implementation of your own.",null],[2259,"Virtualization reduces the number of DOM elements the browser and React have to work with. That reduces the cost of rendering, layout and repaint. The amount of work done on state updates is reduced too. As a result the interface becomes faster and more stable.","An interface\'s performance is limited not only by JavaScript but by the browser\'s work with the DOM.\\n\\nThe main bottlenecks without virtualization\\nWith a large list:\\n- thousands of DOM nodes are created\\n- the browser spends time on layout\\n- every re-render touches a large fragment of the tree\\n\\nEven if the items are not visible, they take part in the computations.\\n\\nWhat exactly virtualization optimizes\\n\\n- The number of DOM nodes\\n- only the visible part is rendered\\n- the rest is replaced by empty spacers\\n\\n- React\'s work\\n- fewer components take part in reconciliation\\n- the comparison of the virtual DOM goes faster\\n\\n- The browser\'s work\\n- less layout and paint\\n- less memory for the DOM\\n\\nWhy this is especially noticeable when scrolling\\nScrolling:\\n- happens often\\n- requires fast updates\\n\\nIf the DOM is small, the browser has time to repaint the interface without freezes.\\n\\nThe additional effects\\n\\n- memory consumption drops\\n- the initial render speeds up\\n- 60 FPS is easier to maintain\\n\\nIn short\\nVirtualization improves performance by cutting the amount of DOM and the number of components taking part in the render sharply. That reduces the load both on React and on the browser.",null],[2260,"Angular and Vue are normally considered when you need a more \\"out of the box\\" framework with ready-made conventions. React is a library rather than a fully fledged framework, so it requires you to choose the architecture and the tooling yourself. Angular suits large corporate projects with strict standards. Vue is often chosen for its lower barrier to entry and its gentle learning curve. The choice depends on the scale of the project, on the team and on the architectural requirements.","What fundamentally distinguishes React from Angular and Vue\\nReact was conceived from the start as a library for building UI rather than as a complete solution. That determines its philosophy and its differences from the other approaches.\\n- React provides only the presentation layer\\n- The architecture, the routing, the state and the build are chosen separately\\n- It suits flexible, evolving systems\\n\\nAngular and Vue offer a more integrated approach.\\n\\nWhen it makes sense to consider Angular\\nAngular is most often chosen in the corporate environment, where standards and predictability matter.\\n- Large teams with varying levels of experience\\n- The need for a strict architecture and shared rules\\n- Long-lived projects with formalized processes\\n\\nAngular sets the project\'s structure, the style of working with state and the dependencies straight away.\\n\\nWhen it makes sense to consider Vue\\nVue occupies an intermediate position between React and Angular.\\n- You need a fast start and a low barrier to entry\\n- The project is of medium size\\n- The team wants a framework but without excessive complexity\\n\\nVue lets you build the complexity up gradually without imposing rigid decisions from the very start.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact is chosen when flexibility and control over the architecture matter. Angular — when strict boundaries and standards are needed. Vue — when a balance between simplicity and structure is required.",null],[2261,"A frontend stack is put together around the project\'s tasks and the team\'s constraints. At its base there is normally a framework or a library for the UI. Then tools for state management, routing, working with the API and building are chosen. Testing, linting and CI play an important role. The stack should be not maximal but sufficient for solving the tasks.","The basic principle of putting a stack together\\nA frontend stack is a set of tools, each of which solves a particular problem. Putting it together begins with an understanding of the project\'s requirements.\\n\\nBefore choosing the technologies, the usual questions answered are:\\n- The scale of the application\\n- The project\'s lifespan\\n- The size and the experience of the team\\n- The requirements for performance and maintenance\\n\\nThe main levels of a frontend stack\\n\\n- The UI level\\n- React as the library for building interfaces\\n- The component-based approach and the declarative description of the UI\\n\\n- State management\\n- Components\' local state\\n- Global state for shared data\\n\\n- Navigation\\n- Client-side routing for an SPA\\n- Dividing the application into screens\\n\\n- Working with the API\\n- HTTP clients\\n- Caching and synchronizing the data\\n\\n- The development infrastructure\\n- Building the project\\n- Code-quality checks\\n- Automated tests\\n\\nThe typical logic of the choice\\nTools are added not \\"from a list\\" but as they are needed.\\n- If there is little data — local state is enough\\n- If the navigation is complex — a router is needed\\n- If the team is large — the role of standards and automation grows\\n\\nConclusion\\nA good frontend stack is the minimally sufficient set of tools that solves the project\'s tasks and does not complicate development unnecessarily.",null],[2262,"Defining a technical task begins with a business need. The requirements are then clarified and translated into a technical format. The constraints, the dependencies and the criteria of completion are determined. After that the task goes into the team\'s work. A good definition reduces the amount of rework.","The process of defining technical tasks is needed in order to turn an abstract idea into work that can be implemented.\\n\\nThe general scheme of defining a task\\n\\n- The source of the task\\n- a business need\\n- a user\'s problem\\n- technical debt\\n\\n- Forming the requirements\\n- a description of the expected behaviour\\n- the main usage scenarios\\n- the constraints and the assumptions\\n\\n- Technical detail\\n- an estimate of the complexity\\n- the effect on the existing architecture\\n- whether the API or the UI needs work\\n\\n- The criteria of completion\\n- what counts as a finished task\\n- which checks have to be passed\\n\\nThe frontend developer\'s role\\nThe frontend developer takes part in assessing feasibility and identifies potential risks at an early stage.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe better structured the definition of a task, the more predictable the result and the lower the cost of changes.",null],[2263,"Requirements are formed not by one person but by several roles. The business determines the aim, the designer the visual presentation, the developers the technical constraints. QA helps account for the error scenarios. Working together reduces the risk of incomplete requirements.","Forming requirements is a collective process in which different roles with different areas of responsibility take part.\\n\\nThe main participants\\n\\n- The Product Manager\\n- they formulate the business goals\\n- they set the priorities\\n\\n- The Designer\\n- they work through the user scenarios\\n- they form the visual solutions\\n\\n- The frontend developer\\n- they assess the complexity of the implementation\\n- they identify the technical constraints\\n\\n- The backend developer\\n- they determine the API\'s capabilities\\n- they clarify the data models\\n\\n- QA\\n- they add the negative scenarios\\n- they help form the acceptance criteria\\n\\nWhy the developers\' participation matters\\nIf the developers do not take part in forming the requirements, those requirements often turn out to be unrealistic or contradictory.\\n\\nConclusion\\nGood requirements appear only when every key role works together.",null],[2264,"Requirements can arrive in various forms — from a detailed specification to a verbal description. Mock-ups, user stories or tickets in a tracker are often used. The requirements are not always complete. It is important to be able to ask clarifying questions","In practice requirements are rarely perfectly written up, and they differ from task to task.\\n\\nThe widespread forms of requirements\\n\\n- A technical specification\\n- a detailed description of the logic\\n- clear criteria of completion\\n\\n- Mock-ups and prototypes\\n- a visual presentation of the interface\\n- the main user scenarios\\n\\n- A user story\\n- a description from the user\'s point of view\\n- a focus on the value\\n\\n- Tickets in a tracker\\n- a brief description\\n- links to additional material\\n\\n- Verbal agreements\\n- discussions at meetings\\n- quick decisions\\n\\nThe risks of informal requirements\\n\\n- different readings of the task\\n- missed scenarios\\n- additional fixes at a late stage\\n\\nConclusion\\nA frontend developer has to be able to work with requirements in any form and to turn them into a clear technical implementation.",null],[2265,"If the requirements are unclear, they cannot simply be accepted \\"as is\\". You have to ask clarifying questions and record what has been agreed. It is important to identify the key scenarios and constraints. Where necessary it is worth offering several implementation options. That reduces the risk of rework.","Unclear requirements are an ordinary situation, and a frontend developer\'s job is not only to write code but to help clarify the task.\\n\\nThe main steps with unclear requirements\\n\\n- Clarifying the aim\\n- what problem the task solves\\n- what the expected result is\\n\\n- Identifying the scenarios\\n- the main user path\\n- the edge cases and the errors\\n\\n- Recording the assumptions\\n- what counts as correct behaviour\\n- which points remain open\\n\\n- Offering options\\n- several approaches with their pros and cons\\n- choosing the most suitable solution\\n\\nWhy it matters to record what has been agreed\\nVerbal agreements are easily forgotten or interpreted differently, so it is important to record them in the ticket or in the documentation.\\n\\nConclusion\\nWorking with unclear requirements is about active communication and reducing uncertainty, not about carrying out a task passively.",null],[2266,"Yes, a frontend developer should take part in clarifying the requirements. They understand the interface\'s constraints and the UI\'s behaviour better than anyone. Taking part early reduces the number of technical and product mistakes. That saves the team time. The quality of the result depends directly on that participation.","A frontend developer sits at the junction of the business, the design and the technology, so their contribution to the requirements is critically important.\\n\\nThe reasons for a frontend developer\'s participation\\n\\n- The technical constraints\\n- the impossibility or the cost of particular solutions\\n- the effect on performance and on the UX\\n\\n- The practice of implementation\\n- an understanding of the complexity of particular scenarios\\n- the identification of hidden dependencies\\n\\n- Protecting the quality of the interface\\n- preventing awkward or contradictory decisions\\n- improving the user experience\\n\\nWhat happens without the frontend developer\'s participation\\n\\n- the requirements turn out not to be implementable\\n- the number of fixes grows\\n- the technical debt increases\\n\\nConclusion\\nA frontend developer\'s participation in clarifying the requirements is an investment in the quality and the stability of the product.",null],[2267,"A frontend developer takes part in the choice of technologies when the decision directly affects the UI and the performance. That is especially important at the start of a project or during major changes. Participation is also necessary when choosing libraries and tools. A developer\'s practical experience helps you avoid poor decisions.","The choice of technologies is not only an architectural decision but a practical question of implementation.\\n\\nThe situations in which a frontend developer\'s participation is necessary\\n\\n- The start of a new project\\n- the choice of framework\\n- the basic frontend stack\\n\\n- Substantial changes to the architecture\\n- a move to new state management or routing\\n- introducing SSR or micro-frontends\\n\\n- The choice of libraries\\n- UI libraries\\n- tools for working with state\\n\\n- Problems with the current solutions\\n- performance\\n- the difficulty of maintenance\\n\\nThe frontend developer\'s role\\nA frontend developer assesses not only a technology\'s theoretical capabilities but the real cost of introducing and maintaining it.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA frontend developer should take part in the choice of technologies, since it is precisely they who bear responsibility for the quality and the resilience of the product\'s UI part.",null],[2268,"Unit tests check individual functions or components in isolation. Integration tests check the interaction between several parts of the system. E2e tests check the whole application from the user\'s point of view. Each kind of test solves its own problem and does not replace the others. A sensible testing strategy uses every level.","Testing in frontend is divided into levels, each of which is responsible for its own area.\\n\\nUnit tests\\nUnit tests focus on the smallest parts of the system.\\n- they check individual functions, hooks or components\\n- they work in isolation\\n- they use mocks and stubs\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe main value of unit tests is fast feedback and a precise indication of where the bug is.\\n\\nIntegration tests\\nIntegration tests check how several parts work together.\\n- a component + a hook\\n- a component + an API client\\n- the interaction of child components\\n\\nThey let you make sure the logic does not break when the parts of the system are combined.\\n\\nE2e tests\\nE2e tests check the whole application.\\n- the user\'s scenarios\\n- real navigation\\n- the interaction with the backend or with an imitation of it\\n\\nSuch tests are the slowest and the most expensive, but they give maximum confidence.\\n\\nHow to choose the kind of test\\n\\n- complex logic — unit\\n- the interaction of components — integration\\n- critical user scenarios — e2e\\n\\nConclusion\\nDifferent kinds of test solve different problems. A reliable testing system is built on a balance of every level.","expect(sum(2, 3)).toBe(5)"],[2269,"Components with an API are tested by mocking the network requests. It is important to check the loading, the successful response and the error states. The tests should not depend on a real backend. What is normally checked is the UI\'s behaviour rather than the details of how the requests are made. That makes the tests stable.","When testing components it is important to check the interface\'s behaviour rather than the HTTP request itself.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Mocking the API\\n- replacing the real requests\\n- controlling the server\'s responses\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- Checking the states\\n- loading\\n- success\\n- error\\n\\nThat lets you make sure the UI responds to the various scenarios correctly.\\n\\nWhat exactly is worth testing\\n\\n- the display of the loader\\n- the rendering of the data once the response arrives\\n- the handling of errors\\n- repeated requests when the data is refreshed\\n\\nWhat you should not do\\n\\n- call a real backend\\n- check the API client\'s internal implementation\\n- make the tests depend on timing\\n\\nIntegration vs unit tests\\n\\n- unit — they check the handling of the response\\n- integration — they check the combination of the component + the API\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe testing of API interactions should be isolated, predictable and oriented towards the UI\'s behaviour.","mockApi.get.mockResolvedValue({ data: [] })"],[2270,"A React component can have local state, props and derived state. The loading, error and empty-result states are also distinguished. Some states exist only logically, without being held in state. Understanding these types helps you design components properly.","A component\'s state is not only `useState` but a broader notion.\\n\\nThe main kinds of state\\n\\n- Local state\\n- it is held inside the component\\n- it governs the UI and the behaviour\\n\\n- Incoming state\\n- it arrives through props\\n- it is controlled by the parent\\n\\n- Derived state\\n- it is computed from the props or the state\\n- it is not held directly\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe UI\'s logical states\\nIn many components the typical states can be distinguished:\\n- loading\\n- success\\n- error\\n- empty\\n\\nThey help structure the rendering and make the code easier to read.\\n\\nThe anti-patterns\\n\\n- duplicating props in state\\n- holding computed values\\n- an excessive number of flags\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the types of state properly helps you make components simpler, more predictable and easier to maintain.","const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)\\n\\nconst isEmpty = items.length === 0"],[2271,"`useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously after the DOM has changed but before the UI is displayed to the user. That lets you read the sizes of elements and make changes at once without any visual flicker. Unlike `useEffect`, it blocks the paint. It has to be used with care. It is normally needed for working with layout and measurements.","`useLayoutEffect` is intended for situations where it matters to affect the DOM before the browser paints a frame.\\n\\nHow useLayoutEffect works\\nThe lifecycle looks like this:\\n- React updates the DOM\\n- `useLayoutEffect` runs\\n- the browser paints the screen\\n\\nThanks to that the user does not see the interface\'s intermediate states.\\n\\nIn which cases it is needed\\n\\n- Measuring the sizes of elements\\n- `getBoundingClientRect`\\n- computing positions\\n\\n- Synchronous layout corrections\\n- correcting the scroll\\n- eliminating interface jumps\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe difference from useEffect\\n\\n- `useEffect` does not block the render\\n- `useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously\\n- using it incorrectly can make performance worse\\n\\nWhen it should not be used\\n\\n- for API requests\\n- for logic unrelated to the DOM\\n- by default, \\"just in case\\"\\n\\nConclusion\\n`useLayoutEffect` is for targeted control of the UI\'s display. It is used only when `useEffect` leads to visual artefacts.","useLayoutEffect(() => {\\n  const height = ref.current.offsetHeight\\n  // using the size before the paint\\n}, [])"],[2272,"Visual artefacts most often arise from incorrect state or from asynchronous updates. You have to identify the moment at which the UI ends up in an inconsistent state. Dividing the states properly and conditional rendering often help. In some cases `useLayoutEffect` is used. It is important to eliminate the cause rather than to mask the effect.","Visual artefacts show themselves as flicker, as elements jumping, or as incorrect data being displayed briefly.\\n\\nThe main causes\\n\\n- Incorrect states\\n- the absence of a loading state\\n- mixing data and UI flags\\n\\n- Asynchronous updates\\n- the data is updated after the render\\n- intermediate values end up in the UI\\n\\n- Layout errors\\n- a sharp change in the sizes of elements\\n- positions being recomputed after the render\\n\\nThe approaches to eliminating them\\n\\n- Explicit UI states\\n- loading\\n- error\\n- empty\\n\\n- Conditional rendering\\n\\n- Preventing unnecessary re-renders\\n- memoization\\n- correct effect dependencies\\n\\n- Working with the layout before the paint\\n- using `useLayoutEffect` where necessary\\n\\nThe anti-patterns\\n\\n- hiding the artefacts behind timers\\n- CSS hacks without eliminating the cause\\n- redundant state flags\\n\\nConclusion\\nVisual artefacts are a symptom of architectural problems. Eliminating them begins with managing state and rendering properly.","if (isLoading) return <Loader />"],[2273,"The logic for updating data is built around the source of truth and the UI\'s states. It is important to account for loading, for errors and for whether the data is current. The updates should be predictable and minimal. A separation between fetching the data and displaying it is often used. That makes maintenance and testing easier.","Designing the logic for updating data is a key aspect of a stable interface.\\n\\nDetermining the source of truth\\nFirst you determine where the data is held:\\n- a component\'s local state\\n- global state\\n- a query cache\\n\\nThe way the UI is updated depends on that.\\n\\nThe typical data states\\nFor most screens the basic states can be distinguished:\\n- loading\\n- success\\n- error\\n- empty\\n\\nThey should be reflected explicitly in the code.\\n\\nThe approaches to updating data\\n\\n- A response to the user\'s actions\\n- submitting a form\\n- clicking a button\\n\\n- A response to changes in the environment\\n- a change of parameters\\n- navigation\\n\\n- A background update\\n- refetching the data\\n- synchronization\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe frequent mistakes\\n\\n- mixing the UI logic and the data logic\\n- the absence of explicit states\\n- manual synchronization instead of a reactive one\\n\\nConclusion\\nWell-designed logic for updating data makes a UI predictable and resilient to change.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData()\\n}, [params])"],[2274,"When the context is incomplete it is important first to determine exactly what is missing. You have to ask clarifying questions and record your assumptions. Analysing a task begins with the aim rather than with the implementation. If the context is not available at once, the work is done iteratively. That reduces the risk of wrong decisions.","Incomplete context is a normal situation, especially at the early stages of a task.\\n\\nThe steps of analysing a task\\n\\n- Determining the aim\\n- what problem has to be solved\\n- what counts as a successful result\\n\\n- Identifying the unknowns\\n- the data\\n- the scenarios\\n- the constraints\\n\\n- Formulating the assumptions\\n- what is accepted provisionally\\n- which decisions may change\\n\\n- Communication\\n- questions to the product side\\n- agreeing the options\\n\\nThe iterative approach\\nIf the whole context cannot be obtained at once:\\n- a minimal version is implemented\\n- the possibility of changes is allowed for\\n- a rigid tie to the details is avoided\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- trying to \\"guess\\" the requirements\\n- ignoring the uncertainty\\n- implementing without recording the assumptions\\n\\nConclusion\\nWorking well with incomplete context means deliberate assumptions, active communication and flexible decisions.",null],[2275,"Yes, it is important for a frontend developer to understand the basics of UX/UI. It helps them implement a design properly and spot problems with the interface. A knowledge of UX/UI improves communication with the designers. It lets them make better technical decisions. That directly affects how comfortable the product is to use.","A frontend developer implements the interface, so a basic understanding of UX/UI improves the quality of the result substantially.\\n\\nWhy a frontend developer needs UX/UI\\n\\n- Implementing the design properly\\n- an understanding of the screens\' logic\\n- observing the hierarchy of the elements\\n\\n- Preventing mistakes\\n- identifying awkward scenarios\\n- spotting contradictions in the mock-ups\\n\\n- Deliberate technical decisions\\n- choosing the right interactions\\n- the effect on performance and on perception\\n\\nThe basic knowledge that is useful\\n\\n- user scenarios\\n- visual hierarchy\\n- accessibility\\n- the interface\'s feedback\\n\\nThe boundary of responsibility\\nA frontend developer does not replace a designer but helps make the interface comfortable and implementable.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA knowledge of the basics of UX/UI makes a frontend developer stronger and increases the value of their contribution to the product.",null],[2276,"Without a finished design, the work begins with an understanding of the user\'s scenarios. Simple prototypes and basic UI components are used. It is important to agree on a minimum level of interface quality. The decisions are made iteratively. This approach lets you move forward without being blocked.","The absence of a design does not mean development stops, but it does call for a different way of organizing the work.\\n\\nWhere to start\\n\\n- Determining the scenarios\\n- what actions the user performs\\n- what states of the screen are possible\\n\\n- A minimal UI\\n- basic components\\n- standard interface patterns\\n\\n- Using a design system\\n- ready-made elements\\n- a single visual style\\n\\nIterative development\\n\\n- the functionality first\\n- then an improvement of the visual part\\n- a gradual refinement of the details\\n\\nThe risks and how to reduce them\\n\\n- record that the solutions are provisional\\n- synchronize with the product and design sides regularly\\n- avoid premature optimization\\n\\nConclusion\\nWork without a design is possible if you focus on the scenarios and improve the interface stage by stage rather than on the final appearance.",null],[2277,"When the processes are minimally formalized it is important to rely on common sense and transparent communication. Tasks have to be recorded in writing, even if they are discussed verbally. The decisions are made iteratively with fast feedback. Responsibility for quality falls partly on the developers. This approach calls for self-reliance.","Minimal formalization is often found in start-ups and small teams.\\n\\nThe basic principles of the work\\n\\n- Recording what has been agreed\\n- a brief description of the task\\n- the expected result\\n- the assumptions\\n\\n- Small iterations\\n- small changes\\n- a fast result\\n- frequent feedback\\n\\n- Transparency of the decisions\\n- an explanation of the approach chosen\\n- a record of the reasons for the decisions\\n\\nThe frontend developer\'s role\\nIn such conditions a frontend developer:\\n- identifies the risks themselves\\n- suggests improvements\\n- helps shape the processes\\n\\nThe main risks\\n\\n- a different understanding of the task\\n- the accumulation of technical debt\\n- chaotic changes\\n\\nConclusion\\nWhen the processes are weak, communication, recording the decisions and an iterative approach become the key things.",null],[2278,"An MVP is developed with a focus on the product\'s key value. The technical decisions should be simple and reversible. There is no point in building a complex architecture in advance. It is important to keep control of the technical debt. An MVP is a way of testing a hypothesis, not a product\'s final version.","Developing an MVP calls for deliberate simplification and prioritization.\\n\\nThe main principles of developing an MVP\\n\\n- A focus on the value\\n- minimal functionality\\n- the key user scenarios\\n\\n- Simple technical decisions\\n- a minimum of dependencies\\n- a clear architecture\\n\\n- Control of the technical debt\\n- deliberate provisional solutions\\n- a record of the limitations\\n\\n- Readiness for change\\n- the absence of rigid connections\\n- the possibility of rewriting parts of the code\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- trying to make it \\"perfect\\"\\n- premature optimization\\n- a complex architecture built without need\\n\\nConclusion\\nA technically sound MVP is a balance between development speed and the possibility of developing it further without rewriting the whole application.",null],[2279,"To analyse React\'s performance, the browser\'s built-in tools and React\'s special tools are used. The React DevTools Profiler and Chrome DevTools Performance are the most common choices. They let you understand which components render too often and why. Logging, `why-did-you-render` and measurements of execution time are also used. These tools help you identify unnecessary renders and heavy operations.","Performance problems in React are usually related to unnecessary re-renders or to heavy computations. There are several levels of tools for finding them.\\n\\nThe React DevTools Profiler\\nThe Profiler is the main tool for analysing React applications:\\n- it shows which components render\\n- it measures each component\'s render time\\n- it lets you compare different commits\\n\\nIt helps you answer the question of why a component updated and how long that took.\\n\\nChrome DevTools\\nThe browser\'s tools give a lower-level view:\\n- the Performance tab shows the CPU load and the FPS\\n- the Memory tab helps you find memory leaks\\n- the Network tab reveals slow requests\\n\\nThe additional tools\\nFor deeper analysis you can use:\\n- `why-did-you-render`, for finding unnecessary renders\\n- manual measurements through `console.time`\\n- React Strict Mode, for revealing side effects\\n\\nConclusion\\nEffective performance analysis calls for a combination of React-specific and browser tools. It is worth starting with the Profiler and then going deeper into Chrome DevTools where necessary.",null],[2280,"The initialization of data begins once the root component has mounted. Normally the data is loaded in a `useEffect` with an empty array of dependencies. The data received is saved in state or in a global store. Until the loading has finished, a loading state may be displayed. This process determines the application\'s first render.","When starting a React application it is important to determine the moment and the manner of loading the initial data correctly, so that the interface is predictable.\\n\\nThe initial render\\nAfter `ReactDOM.createRoot().render()` is called, what happens is:\\n- the root component renders\\n- the local state is initialized\\n- the effects run after the first render\\n\\nAt this stage the data has not usually been loaded yet.\\n\\nLoading the data\\n`useEffect` is the usual choice:\\n\\nSuch an effect runs once, after the component has mounted.\\n\\nWhere the data is held\\nDepending on the architecture, the data can be saved:\\n- in the component\'s local state\\n- in a context\\n- in a global store — Redux, for example\\n\\nThe loading state\\nIn practice an additional piece of state is almost always used:\\n- a loading flag\\n- error handling\\n- the display of placeholders or spinners\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe initialization of data in React normally happens after the first render, through effects. Organizing this process well directly affects both the UX and the application\'s architecture.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData()\\n}, [])"],[2281,"The loading of data when a component mounts is normally done by means of `useEffect`. An effect with an empty array of dependencies runs once, after the first render. Inside the effect an asynchronous data-loading function is called. The result is saved in the component\'s state. This approach is the standard one for React.","When a component mounts React does not yet know about the external data, so its loading is moved into side effects.\\n\\nThe basic approach\\nThe most widespread way is to use `useEffect`:\\n\\nThis effect runs only once, immediately after the component has been added to the DOM.\\n\\nThe asynchronous logic\\nSince `useEffect` cannot be asynchronous directly, a helper function is normally used:\\n\\nManaging the states\\nWhen loading data it is important to allow for several states:\\n- the initial state\\n- the loading state\\n- the data being received successfully\\n- an error in the request\\n\\nThat lets you avoid an incorrect UI and makes the application more resilient.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe loading of data when a component mounts should be done through effects. A clear separation between the loading logic and the render makes the code predictable and convenient to maintain.","useEffect(() => {\\n  loadData()\\n}, [])\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const fetchData = async () => {\\n    const response = await fetch(\'/api/data\')\\n    const result = await response.json()\\n    setData(result)\\n  }\\n\\n  fetchData()\\n}, [])"],[2282,"The asynchronous initialization of state can lead to inconsistent data. Situations are possible where the component has already unmounted while an attempt is made to update its state. Races between requests and unnecessary repaints also arise. Handling errors incorrectly leads to a broken UI. These problems call for explicit control of the lifecycle.","Loading data asynchronously looks simple, but in practice it can cause a number of complicated problems.\\n\\nUpdating state after the unmount\\nIf the component was removed before the request finished:\\n- `setState` may run all the same\\n- warnings and memory leaks arise\\n\\nThis is often solved with a cancellation flag or an `AbortController`.\\n\\nRaces between requests\\nWhen effects are called repeatedly, a situation is possible in which:\\n- the old request finishes later than the new one\\n- the state is updated with stale data\\n\\nThis happens especially often when the parameters or the ID change.\\n\\nIntermediate states\\nAsynchronous initialization means that:\\n- the data is temporarily `null` or `undefined`\\n- the components can crash without defensive checks\\n\\nConditional renders are therefore often used.\\n\\nUnnecessary re-renders\\nOrganizing the effects incorrectly can cause:\\n- repeated requests\\n- cascading state updates\\n- degraded performance\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe asynchronous initialization of state calls for careful control of the lifecycle, of the effect dependencies and of error handling. Without that the application becomes unstable and hard to maintain.",null],[2283,"SSR is the rendering of a page on the server before the HTML is sent to the browser.\\nSearch engines receive ready-made HTML content straight away rather than a blank page with JavaScript.\\nThat speeds indexing up and reduces the risk of the content not being indexed.\\nSSR matters especially for sites with dynamic content and a high dependence on SEO.","Search crawlers analyse the HTML document they receive when they request a page. The way that HTML is generated directly affects SEO.\\n\\nA definition\\nServer-Side Rendering (SSR) is the approach in which the HTML page is formed on the server and sent to the client with its content already in place.\\n\\nThe problem with client-side rendering\\nWith Client-Side Rendering (CSR):\\n- The server returns almost empty HTML\\n- The content appears only once the JavaScript has run\\n- The search crawler may:\\n- not wait for the JS to run\\n- index an empty page\\n\\nSSR\'s advantages for SEO\\nThe use of SSR gives the following benefits:\\n- The search engine sees the content immediately\\n- Indexing happens faster\\n- The First Contentful Paint metric improves\\n- The risk of SEO problems in an SPA is reduced\\n\\nAn example scenario\\n\\n- A blog, a news site, a product catalogue\\n- The content matters to the search results\\n- SSR or hybrid rendering is used\\n\\nConclusion\\nSSR matters for SEO because it guarantees that the content is available to the search engines. It is especially useful for public pages with dynamic data.",null],[2284,"`props.children` is used to pass nested JSX into a component.\\nIt lets a component be a wrapper for arbitrary content.\\nIt is the main mechanism of composition in React.\\n`children` can contain elements, strings or other components.","React is built around the idea of composition, and `props.children` is the key tool for implementing it.\\n\\nA definition\\nprops.children is a special property containing everything that was passed between a component\'s opening and closing tags.\\n\\nHow props.children works\\nWhen the component is used:\\n\\nReact will pass that content into the `Card` component\'s `props.children` automatically.\\n\\nThe typical usage scenarios\\n`props.children` is used when a component:\\n- Is a container or a layout component\\n- Has to be reusable\\n- Does not know the structure of the nested content in advance\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe characteristics\\n\\n- `children` can be:\\n- a single element\\n- an array of elements\\n- `null`\\n\\n- To work with children, `React.Children` is used\\n\\nConclusion\\n`props.children` is the foundation of composition in React. It lets you create flexible, reusable components without a rigid tie to the content.","<Card>\\n  <h2>A heading</h2>\\n  <p>Some text</p>\\n</Card>\\n\\nfunction Card({ children }) {\\n  return <div className=\\"card\\">{children}</div>;\\n}"],[2285,"JSX compiles into ordinary JavaScript.\\nMost often it is turned into `React.createElement` calls.\\nThe browser does not understand JSX directly.\\nJSX is nothing more than syntactic sugar.","JSX is not a JavaScript standard and cannot be executed by the browser without being transformed first.\\n\\nA definition\\nJSX is a syntax extension to JavaScript that makes describing the structure of a UI easier.\\n\\nWhat happens during compilation\\nAt build time (Babel, SWC):\\n- The JSX is turned into JavaScript\\n- Every JSX element becomes a function call\\n- In React that is normally `React.createElement`\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nAfter compilation:\\n\\nThe modern JSX runtime\\nIn the newer versions of React an automatic runtime is used:\\n- No `React` import is required\\n- The JSX compiles into calls to functions from `react/jsx-runtime`\\n\\nWhy this is needed\\n\\n- The browser executes only JavaScript\\n- JSX makes the code more readable\\n- The compilation happens before the application runs\\n\\nConclusion\\nJSX is always compiled into JavaScript. It is syntactic sugar that makes describing a UI easier but does not exist at run time.","<h1>Hello</h1>\\n\\nReact.createElement(\\"h1\\", null, \\"Hello\\");"],[2286,"A `ref` is used to hold mutable values.\\nChanges to `ref.current` are not tracked by React.\\nA re-render is caused only by a change of state or props.\\nA `ref` takes no part in the render process.","React has a clear separation between the data that affects the UI and auxiliary values.\\n\\nA definition\\nA ref is an object with a `current` field, intended for holding values that should not cause a re-render.\\n\\nWhy a ref does not cause a re-render\\nReact does not track changes to `ref.current`, because:\\n- a ref takes no part in reconciliation\\n- a ref is intended for imperative operations\\n- an automatic re-render would reduce performance\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhen to use a ref\\nA `ref` is used for:\\n- access to DOM elements\\n- holding timers and ids\\n- keeping previous values\\n- working with external libraries\\n\\nA comparison with state\\n\\n- `state`:\\n- it causes a re-render\\n- it is used for the UI\\n\\n- `ref`:\\n- it causes no re-render\\n- it is used for auxiliary data\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `ref` does not cause a re-render because it is not intended for managing the UI. If a change has to be reflected in the interface, `state` has to be used.","const countRef = useRef(0);\\n\\ncountRef.current += 1; // no re-render happens"],[2287,"A library of shared UI components is a separate npm package containing reusable React components, styles and utilities. It is developed as an independent project, built and published to a registry. Applications include it as an ordinary dependency through package.json. When the library\'s version is updated, the projects receive the new components or changes. This lets you manage the UI centrally and reduce code duplication.","A definition\\nA UI component library is an isolated frontend package of visual components with the styles and the logic associated with them, intended for reuse in different projects.\\n\\nHow such a library is put together\\nBefore using a library it is important to understand its internal structure and its lifecycle. It normally comprises the following parts:\\n- React components (Button, Input, Modal and so on)\\n- Shared styles or design tokens\\n- TypeScript types\\n- An entry point for the build and the exports\\n\\nHow it is included\\nOnce published to npm, the library is used as an ordinary dependency:\\n\\nThe application\'s bundler pulls the library\'s code from node_modules and includes it in the final bundle.\\n\\nHow the library is updated\\nThe process normally looks like this:\\n- The changes are made in the library\'s repository\\n- The package\'s version is raised\\n- The package is published to npm\\n- The projects update the dependency and receive the changes\\n\\nIn short\\nMoving UI components into an npm package lets you centralize the UI, make maintenance easier and ensure several applications share a single appearance.","import { Button } from \'@company/ui-kit\'"],[2288,"Moving components into a separate library is for reusing the UI between projects. It reduces code duplication and makes maintenance easier. Changes to the components can be made in one place. The teams get a single design and predictable behaviour from the elements. The result is faster development and fewer bugs.","The basic idea\\nWhen several projects use the same buttons, forms and modal windows, it makes sense to move them into a shared place rather than copying the code by hand.\\n\\nThe main advantages\\nThis approach gives several practical benefits:\\n- A single UI across every product\\n- Less copy-paste and fewer divergences in the implementation\\n- Changes introduced quickly across every project at once\\n- Easier onboarding for new developers\\n\\nThe effect on teamwork\\nHaving a shared library:\\n- makes code review easier, since the components are standardized\\n- reduces the number of UI bugs\\n- lets the frontend team develop the design systematically\\n\\nWhen this is especially useful\\nA separate library is justified if:\\n- there are 2 or more frontend projects\\n- a shared design is used\\n- different teams work on the products\\n\\nIn short\\nA shared component library is a tool for scaling frontend development and for controlling the quality of the UI.",null],[2289,"The maintenance of a library is built around versioning and backwards compatibility. Semantic versioning is the usual choice. The changes are documented and published through releases. The consuming projects update the version deliberately. That lets you control the effect of the changes on the products.","A definition\\nVersioning a library is the process of managing a package\'s changes so that the consuming projects understand whether it is safe to upgrade.\\n\\nSemantic Versioning\\nsemver is the most common choice:\\n- major — breaking changes\\n- minor — new capabilities with nothing broken\\n- patch — bug fixes\\n\\nMaintenance and development\\nFor a library to work stably, the usual practice is:\\n- a changelog is kept\\n- the breaking changes are described\\n- pull requests and code review are used\\n- visual or unit tests for the components are added\\n\\nCompatibility with the projects\\nTo minimize problems:\\n- breaking changes are released rarely\\n- obsolete APIs are first marked as deprecated\\n- time is allowed for the migration\\n\\nIn short\\nSensible versioning and transparent releases make a shared library safe and convenient to use across several projects.",null],[2290,"Screenshot tests are tests that compare an interface\'s current appearance against a reference image. If the appearance has changed, the test reports the differences. This approach helps you detect visual bugs — broken layout or incorrect styles, for example. Tools such as Storybook, Playwright or Percy are the usual choices.","Screenshot tests are a way of checking a UI automatically, in which the system takes a snapshot of a component or a page and compares it against a saved reference.\\n\\nHow the mechanism works\\n\\n- The component or the page is rendered.\\n- A screenshot is taken.\\n- The screenshot is compared against the baseline image.\\n- If the differences exceed the permitted threshold, the test fails.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Checking a design system\'s components\\n- Keeping control of regressions after CSS changes\\n- Checking responsiveness\\n\\nAn example (a notional Playwright example)\\n\\nThe pros\\n\\n- It lets you detect visual bugs that are not visible in unit tests\\n- It suits UI libraries well\\n\\nThe cons\\n\\n- It is sensitive to small changes\\n- It requires the baseline screenshots to be maintained\\n\\nConclusion\\nScreenshot tests are useful for keeping control of visual changes and are especially effective when developing components and design systems.","await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot(\\"button.png\\");"],[2291,"The state can be saved in LocalStorage, in cookies, in IndexedDB or on the server. In frontend applications LocalStorage together with Redux or React state is the common choice. When the application loads, the data is read from the store and restored into the state. That lets you preserve the authorization, the settings and a form.","Preserving the state between reloads is necessary when it matters that the user not lose their data after the page refreshes.\\n\\nThe main ways\\n\\n- LocalStorage\\nIt is suited to:\\n\\n- tokens\\n- settings\\n- small amounts of data\\n\\n- SessionStorage\\nUsed if the data should live only within the tab.\\n- IndexedDB\\nIt is suited to:\\n\\n- large volumes of data\\n- offline applications\\n\\n- Saving on the server\\nUsed for:\\n\\n- user data\\n- carts\\n- profiles\\n\\nThe frequent pattern in React\\n\\n- Read the data when the application initializes\\n- Put it in the state or the store\\n- Save it when it changes\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor simple tasks LocalStorage is the most common choice, and for large amounts of data or an offline mode — IndexedDB or the server.","localStorage.setItem(\\"theme\\", \\"dark\\");\\nconst theme = localStorage.getItem(\\"theme\\");"],[2292,"API requests are normally moved into a separate layer and performed through fetch or axios. In frontend applications hooks, services or libraries for working with server state are used. That makes reuse and the management of the loading and error states easier.","Working with an API in frontend calls for a structured approach, so that the code is maintainable.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- A service layer\\nThe requests are moved into separate functions.\\n\\n- The use of hooks\\nThe requests are encapsulated in custom hooks.\\n\\n- Libraries for server state\\n\\n- React Query\\n- SWR\\n\\nThey handle automatically:\\n- the cache\\n- repeated requests\\n- errors\\n\\nWhat is normally taken into account\\n\\n- error handling\\n- the loading states\\n- cancelling requests\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe best practice is considered to be moving the requests into a separate layer and using libraries for managing server state.","export const getUsers = () => fetch(\\"/api/users\\");\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  loadUsers();\\n}, []);"],[2293,"In React a component re-renders when its state or props change. If you need to force a re-render, you can change the state, use `useReducer`, change the component\'s `key`, or in class components call `forceUpdate`. A direct forced re-render is normally considered undesirable practice.","In React a re-render is started when the state or the input data changes. Sometimes it has to be initiated by hand.\\n\\nThe main ways\\n\\n- Changing the state\\nThe most correct way is to change the state.\\n\\n- Using useReducer\\nIt is suited to simply triggering an update.\\n\\n- Changing the key\\nReact will recreate the component.\\n\\n- forceUpdate (class components)\\n\\nWhen this is needed\\n\\n- updating data not directly related to the state\\n- integration with third-party libraries\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe most correct way of causing a re-render is to change the state; the direct methods are used rarely and in special cases.","setTick(t => t + 1);\\n\\nconst [, forceUpdate] = useReducer(x => x + 1, 0);\\n\\n<Component key={id} />\\n\\nthis.forceUpdate();"],[2294,"React tracks changes to state and props, not to ordinary variables. If you change an ordinary variable, React will not learn about it and will not start a repeated render. To update the interface you have to use `useState` or another state-management mechanism. That lets React control the updates and optimize the rendering.","React renders components from state and props rather than from any variable.\\n\\n1) How React understands that it needs to update\\nReact responds to:\\n- A change of state.\\n- A change of props.\\n- Sometimes — a change of context.\\n\\nOrdinary variables are not tracked.\\n\\n2) An example of the problem\\n\\nThe reason:\\n- React does not know that the variable has changed.\\n- No re-render is started.\\n\\n3) The right approach\\nUsing state:\\n\\n4) Why React is built this way\\n\\n- Managed updates.\\n- Predictability.\\n- Performance optimization.\\n\\nIf React tracked any variable:\\n- It would be very expensive.\\n- The behaviour would become unstable.\\n\\nConclusion: React starts a re-render only when state or props change, so ordinary variables are not suited to holding interface data","let count = 0;\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  function handleClick() {\\n    count++;\\n    console.log(count); // it changes, but the UI does not\\n  }\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>{count}</button>;\\n}\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  setCount(count + 1);\\n}"],[2295,"A list of items is normally held in state as an array. When updating it you must not change the array directly; you have to create a new one. That lets React determine the changes properly and update the interface. For that, the spread operator or methods that return a new array are used.","In React it is important to observe the principle of the immutability of state.\\n\\n1) Holding a list\\nAn array is the usual choice:\\n\\n2) Why the array must not be changed directly\\nWrong:\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- The reference to the array does not change.\\n- React may miss the update.\\n\\n3) The right way\\nCreating a new array:\\n\\nRemoving:\\n\\nUpdating:\\n\\n4) Why this matters\\n\\n- React compares references.\\n- That speeds the diffing up.\\n- The code becomes predictable.\\n\\nConclusion: a list in React has to be held in state and updated by creating a new array, so that React determines the changes properly.","const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\\n\\nitems.push(newItem);\\nsetItems(items); // React may not notice the change\\n\\nsetItems([...items, newItem]);\\n\\nsetItems(items.filter(i => i.id !== id));\\n\\nsetItems(items.map(i =>\\n  i.id === id ? { ...i, name: \\"New\\" } : i\\n));"],[2296,"The `key` attribute helps React determine which items of a list have changed, been added or been removed. That speeds the updating of the interface up. Without a `key` React may match the elements up incorrectly. A unique identifier is the usual choice.","React uses a virtual-DOM comparison algorithm in order to update only the elements that have changed.\\n\\nA definition: a `key` is a list item\'s unique identifier, which helps React match the elements up correctly between renders.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n- React finds the changes faster.\\n- Unnecessary repaints are prevented.\\n- The components\' state is preserved.\\n\\nA bad example\\n\\nAn index can change when the list is sorted or items are removed.\\n\\nWhen an index may be used\\n- The list is static.\\n- The items do not change.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA `key` is needed for a list to update correctly and efficiently, and it is better to use stable unique identifiers.","items.map(item => (\\n  <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n));\\n\\nkey={index}"],[2297,"To get the previous state, a function is passed to `setState` or `setCount`. That function receives the previous value of the state. This way guarantees the update is correct, especially with several calls in a row. It is standard practice in React.","React can perform state updates asynchronously, so using the old value directly sometimes leads to bugs.\\n\\nA definition: an updater function is a function that receives the previous state and returns the new one.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n- Updates can be combined.\\n- The state\'s value can go stale.\\n- The function guarantees the current value.\\n\\nAn example of the mistake\\n\\nBoth calls may use one and the same value.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn updater function is the reliable way of working with the previous state and of avoiding bugs with asynchronous updates.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nsetCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n\\nsetCount(count + 1);\\nsetCount(count + 1);"],[2298,"To make `useEffect` run only once, you have to pass an empty array of dependencies `[]`. That means the effect depends on no values and will run only after the first render. This approach is often used for loading data or for initialization. There will be no repeated calls as long as the dependencies do not change.","The `useEffect` hook runs after the render and can run again when its dependencies change.\\n\\nA definition: a dependency array is a list of values on whose change the effect will run again.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHow this works\\n- The component renders for the first time.\\n- React runs the effect.\\n- Since there are no dependencies, no repeated run happens.\\n\\nThe typical usage scenarios\\n- Loading data from the server.\\n- Subscribing to events.\\n- Initializing libraries.\\n\\nAn important point\\n\\nIn Strict Mode in development the effect may be called twice, but that behaviour is only for checking purposes.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn empty array of dependencies lets you run `useEffect` only when the component mounts.","useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"Component mounted\\");\\n}, []);"],[2299,"React Portals let you render a component in a different part of the DOM without changing the structure of the React components. This is often used for modal windows, tooltips and dropdown menus. The approach helps you avoid problems with `z-index` and with containers overflowing. The component\'s logic nevertheless stays where it was.","Sometimes an element has to be displayed outside its parent container while logically remaining inside the component.\\n\\nA definition: a Portal is a way of rendering a React element into an arbitrary DOM node by means of `ReactDOM.createPortal`.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHow this works\\n- The component stays in the React tree.\\n- In the DOM it is placed somewhere else.\\n- The events go on working as usual.\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n- Modal windows.\\n- Tooltips.\\n- Context menus.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\nContainers can have `overflow: hidden` or positioning constraints.\\n\\nConclusion\\nPortals let you display elements over the interface properly without disturbing the components\' architecture.","ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n  <Modal />,\\n  document.getElementById(\\"modal-root\\")\\n);"],[2300,"Storybook is an environment in which UI components are run separately from the application and shown as \\"stories\\" with their various states. It helps you develop components faster, document components\' APIs and check the various variants (loading, error, empty data). It is often used for visual testing and for regressions. It is also convenient for working together with designers and QA. In large projects Storybook reduces the coupling of components to particular pages.","Storybook is useful when you have a great many reusable components and need to keep control of the UI\'s quality without constantly running the whole application.\\n\\nA definition\\nA definition: Storybook is a tool for developing and demonstrating UI components in isolation, where each component is described by a set of stories (state scenarios).\\n\\nWhy it is used in a project\\n\\n- Isolation\\n- A component can be debugged without routing, data or the application\'s complex infrastructure\\n\\n- Documentation\\n- Intuitive examples of the states + a description of the props\\n\\n- Visual checking\\n- Comparing screenshots (visual regression) in order to find UI breakage\\n\\n- Team communication\\n- The designer/QA sees the component \\"as it is\\" and can check its states\\n\\n- A catalogue for the design system\\n- It is convenient for maintaining a UI kit and the rules for using the components\\n\\nAn example of a simple story (React)\\n\\nThe typical practices\\n\\n- Write stories for the key states:\\n- `Default`, `Loading`, `Disabled`, `Error`, `WithIcon`\\n\\n- Substitute the dependencies:\\n- mock data, a mock hook, dummy handlers\\n\\n- Add add-ons:\\n- Controls (to play with the props), Actions (a log of the clicks), Docs\\n\\nConclusion\\nStorybook is worth using when you want to speed component development up, to have living documentation of your UI and to reduce visual regressions when things change.","import type { Meta, StoryObj } from \\"@storybook/react\\";\\nimport { Button } from \\"./Button\\";\\n\\nconst meta: Meta<typeof Button> = { component: Button, title: \\"UI/Button\\" };\\nexport default meta;\\n\\nexport const Primary: StoryObj<typeof Button> = {\\n  args: { children: \\"Save\\", variant: \\"primary\\" }\\n};"]]');export{e as default};